1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,720

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,280
So today, we're going to
talk about evaluation.

3
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:13,400
So far in this class
we've done everything we

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,680
need to train a language model.

5
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:19,920
We defined the architecture,
the optimizer, the main training

6
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:20,540
loop.

7
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:25,160
We showed how to make the
training fast with kernels,

8
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:26,840
parallelism.

9
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,240
And then we saw scaling laws.

10
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:33,560
We saw a little bit of how to
make inference fast as well.

11
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,840
So the only missing piece
now is talking about the data

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,880
that you train on.

13
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:43,360
So we're going to spend next
week talking about that.

14
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,640
And data is going to
shape the model behavior.

15
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:49,520
If you train on
code, then presumably

16
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,380
your model is going
to be good at code.

17
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,240
If you train only
on DNA sequences,

18
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,320
it probably can't speak English.

19
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,920
But before we even
get to data, we

20
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,170
need to talk about what
behavior we want from a model,

21
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:05,489
and that's going to be
the topic of evaluation.

22
00:01:05,489 --> 00:01:08,290
So evaluation asks a
very simple question--

23
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:13,250
given the model that we've
trained, how good is it?

24
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:14,890
So what is good?

25
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:19,770
So evaluation might seem like
a fairly mechanical process.

26
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:21,290
You define some
prompts, you send

27
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:23,870
these prompts to your model,
you get back some responses,

28
00:01:23,870 --> 00:01:25,470
and then you compute
the accuracy.

29
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:29,010
So why do we have a whole
lecture on evaluation?

30
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:32,110
But actually, evaluation
is a very deep topic,

31
00:01:32,110 --> 00:01:34,690
and it's also very important
because evaluation is what

32
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:36,650
shapes the development of AI.

33
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:41,050
Evaluation sets North Stars
and model developers--

34
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:44,690
open, closed-- everyone
looks at evaluation

35
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:49,290
as a measure of progress.

36
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:52,210
So by thinking carefully
about your evaluation,

37
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:54,610
you implicitly shape
the development

38
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:57,790
of what your model is
going to be able to do.

39
00:01:57,790 --> 00:02:01,350
So what's hard about
evaluation is the following.

40
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,190
So often, you start it with
an abstract construct of what

41
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:07,150
do I want my model to do?

42
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:11,630
Maybe I want my model to
be good at conversation

43
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:13,750
or good at reasoning.

44
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:14,250
OK.

45
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:16,230
So those are abstract concepts.

46
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:18,470
And then evaluation
is really the process

47
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:23,030
of turning that into a concrete
metric powered by, presumably,

48
00:02:23,030 --> 00:02:26,670
some concrete prompts
or environments.

49
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:29,930
So this is going to
be the core challenge,

50
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:32,270
and we'll see many
examples of how

51
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:36,190
this process of abstract
construct, concrete metric

52
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:38,070
shows up.

53
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:42,070
So let's go back to a question
of what makes a model good.

54
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,430
Just kind of superficially.

55
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,290
Maybe a model is good if
it does well on benchmarks.

56
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:49,890
So there's this website,
Artificial Analysis,

57
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:54,670
which has become a bit of a
standard for thinking about

58
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:57,200
quote unquote,
"intelligence of models."

59
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,840
So if you go to
Artificial Analysis AI,

60
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,920
you can see a ranking of
different models from GPT-5.5,

61
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:07,240
all the way down-- well, there's
many more models after this

62
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,640
point.

63
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:13,110
And so this is a measure.

64
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:16,000
It includes a bunch of different
data sets, some of which

65
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,360
we'll talk about.

66
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,960
Or maybe a model is good if
it does well on benchmark

67
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:26,160
and is also cheap to run,
because cost should matter.

68
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:30,440
And this plot shows both
the intelligence index

69
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,520
from before, but
against how much money

70
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:38,080
it costs to run a particular
model based on inference cost.

71
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:41,560
And you can see, of course,
as you might expect,

72
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:43,820
that the two are correlated.

73
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:47,860
The more you pay to run, the
higher the intelligence index,

74
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:52,800
but it's not exactly aligned.

75
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,770
But maybe a model is good
if people just prefer it.

76
00:03:57,770 --> 00:04:00,150
Later we'll also go a
little bit more into depth.

77
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:04,010
So this is the take that
Arena AI, formerly known

78
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:06,290
as Chatbot Arena, takes.

79
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:08,570
And you can form a
ranking based on models,

80
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:11,330
based on whether
people like it or not.

81
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:14,690
Or maybe a model is
good if people simply

82
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:17,870
choose to use it, and
therefore, pay for it.

83
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:22,010
So OpenRouter is
this service that

84
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:25,010
allows one to use
multiple different models

85
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:28,952
through a central endpoint.

86
00:04:28,952 --> 00:04:30,410
And as a result,
they collect a lot

87
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:34,410
of statistics of what
models people are using.

88
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:37,810
And they publish
statistics, which

89
00:04:37,810 --> 00:04:39,330
is really interesting
to look at,

90
00:04:39,330 --> 00:04:43,210
the top bottles that are
used through OpenRouter.

91
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:45,650
So now, this is not
representative of all model

92
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:50,410
usage, but it gives you a sense
of what people on open router

93
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,450
are doing.

94
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:53,840
And so this is kind of
more of an economic lens.

95
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:58,000
I don't what good is, but
if people are paying for it,

96
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,660
it must be good.

97
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:02,240
OK.

98
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:07,200
So none of those are
necessarily the correct answer.

99
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,760
I don't know If there
is a correct answer,

100
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,820
but I'm just trying to get you
to think about different ways

101
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:16,060
of thinking about what good is.

102
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:21,180
So let's start now by looking at
different ways, concrete ways,

103
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,300
of evaluating language models.

104
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:26,460
And there's no better place
to start than going back

105
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:30,860
to the core idea of a
language model, which

106
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:36,180
is that it's a distribution P
of x over sequences of tokens.

107
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,620
And through that lens,
there's a very natural way

108
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:41,260
to evaluate a
distribution, which

109
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:46,540
is a perplexity, or
likelihood or log loss.

110
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:49,540
Those are all kind
of related concepts.

111
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:54,240
And it basically says, I
have a test data set d,

112
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:58,680
and I'm going to see how much
probability mass my language

113
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:00,920
model assigns to that d.

114
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:03,360
And I normalize it and
divide so that the numbers

115
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,840
are more interpretable.

116
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,420
But that's basically it.

117
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:11,720
How much mass does my
probability language model

118
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,440
assigned to a data set.

119
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,100
OK, so this is fairly natural.

120
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:19,040
In fact, when we
were doing training,

121
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,600
we minimized the perplexity
on the training set.

122
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,400
So the obvious thing to do
is measure the perplexity

123
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,320
on the test set, and this is
what people traditionally did

124
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:31,280
in language modeling research
for many years throughout

125
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:33,240
the 2010s.

126
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:35,380
If you pick up a
language modeling paper,

127
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:36,640
this is what they did.

128
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:39,840
And back then,
standard data sets

129
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,320
included the Penn
Treebank, WikiText-103.

130
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,200
There was also this One
Billion Word Benchmark,

131
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:53,330
which was taken from
machine translation sources.

132
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:56,650
And this is all in the
classic paradigm, which is

133
00:06:56,650 --> 00:06:58,750
in-distribution of evaluation.

134
00:06:58,750 --> 00:07:02,110
You train on some train
split of that data set,

135
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:07,090
and then you evaluate on some
test split of that data set.

136
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:11,490
And people actually
made a lot of progress,

137
00:07:11,490 --> 00:07:14,450
which was just measured
in perplexity reduction.

138
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:16,490
So for example, there's
this famous paper

139
00:07:16,490 --> 00:07:22,490
from 2016, which
showed that you could

140
00:07:22,490 --> 00:07:27,450
apply CNNs and LSTMs to this
One Billion Word Benchmark

141
00:07:27,450 --> 00:07:31,090
and get a huge
perplexity reduction.

142
00:07:31,090 --> 00:07:33,110
And bear in mind,
around that time,

143
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:35,490
people were talking still
about n-gram models and maybe

144
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:37,650
hybrid models.

145
00:07:37,650 --> 00:07:40,890
And this was the first
definitive result

146
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:46,050
that showed just pure neural
is clearly the way to go.

147
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:48,280
OK, so life was very
simple back then.

148
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:49,640
You train on the train split.

149
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:52,900
You test on a test
split for perplexity.

150
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:58,380
So then when GPT-2 came
around in 2019 from OpenAI,

151
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:01,540
they trained on
the data set called

152
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:04,620
WebText, which was 40
gigabytes of text websites

153
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:07,140
that were linked from Reddit.

154
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:10,920
And the way they evaluated
was actually different.

155
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:16,060
They evaluated zero-shot
on standard data sets

156
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:19,380
that everyone else
was evaluating on.

157
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:21,840
So this is out-of-distribution
evaluation.

158
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:25,820
So this is a table from their
paper, which show that as they

159
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:27,400
train different models.

160
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:30,700
So the biggest model is
1.5 billion parameters.

161
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:36,460
And they were able to show
quite a bit of improvement,

162
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:39,440
especially on the
small data sets.

163
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:42,780
So for example, PTB,
which is a tiny data set.

164
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:46,480
They were getting 35 perplexity
compared to the State

165
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,400
of The Art, which is 46.

166
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:50,820
Now, if you have enough
in-distribution data,

167
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:53,960
you don't quite do
as well as the SOTA.

168
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:58,120
But nonetheless, this
is kind of impressive,

169
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,200
given that this model was not
trained on One Billion Word's

170
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,480
data set at all.

171
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:05,120
I don't know.

172
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,040
Maybe there was some
overlap, because I

173
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:14,320
don't know if they did a
careful train test decontam.

174
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:20,440
And so it ushered
in a new paradigm

175
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:22,920
of thinking about language
models as not just

176
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,040
the in-distribution
setting, but how

177
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:31,480
we wanted to train
on a large data set

178
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:34,560
and evaluate on standard
benchmarks, which nowadays I

179
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:37,300
think has been very
common standard.

180
00:09:37,300 --> 00:09:41,620
But back then, it was seen
as a bit of a novelty.

181
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:46,530

182
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:49,890
I will also argue, since we're
talking about perplexity, that

183
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:53,050
perplexity is you all need.

184
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:54,990
I wouldn't to take
this too seriously,

185
00:09:54,990 --> 00:09:57,250
but I think it is
a mindset, I think,

186
00:09:57,250 --> 00:10:01,130
that drives a lot of
language modeling research.

187
00:10:01,130 --> 00:10:03,810
And the basic argument
goes like this.

188
00:10:03,810 --> 00:10:05,830
So there's some true
distribution out there--

189
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:07,290
let's call it t--

190
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:09,970
and you're training
this model p.

191
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:14,210
So what is the best
perplexity you could obtain?

192
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:17,890
Well, the best
perplexity is the entropy

193
00:10:17,890 --> 00:10:21,850
of that true
distribution, and that

194
00:10:21,850 --> 00:10:26,130
is realized when p equals t.

195
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:30,290
So by minimizing
perplexity, you're basically

196
00:10:30,290 --> 00:10:35,130
pushing down the perplexity.

197
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:38,610
And the only unique minimizer
is when you actually

198
00:10:38,610 --> 00:10:40,780
get the true distribution.

199
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:44,880
And when you have the true
distribution, you're done.

200
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:46,066
You just model everything.

201
00:10:46,066 --> 00:10:48,060
You can solve all
the problems, and you

202
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:50,780
can do things like
condition on the problem,

203
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:52,000
generate a solution.

204
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,740
You can condition on a
question, generate the answer.

205
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:59,140
So by pushing down
on perplexity,

206
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:03,780
we'll eventually reach
AGI, is the argument.

207
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:05,500
I mean, this might
sound, I don't know,

208
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:08,100
either a bit fantastical.

209
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:10,900
But in some sense,
this is really,

210
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:13,260
I think, a strong
driver of many people

211
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:16,980
who have been scaling and
scaling language models

212
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:22,620
at a time when, maybe, the
gains weren't as obvious.

213
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,500
Before GPT-3, I
think it wasn't clear

214
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:29,180
that language models were going
to have such a huge impact.

215
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:33,020
And it was really this belief
that, if you drove perplexity

216
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:36,940
down, good things will happen.

217
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:43,760
So of course, Perplexity
is maybe not all.

218
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:47,760
And one way to frame it is
that perplexity is maybe more

219
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:49,025
than you need.

220
00:11:49,025 --> 00:11:51,400
So for example, if you have
a sentence like "Stanford was

221
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:55,360
founded in 1885,"
perplexity will say,

222
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:59,400
penalize the prediction
on all tokens.

223
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:03,640
So in particular, it'll say,
what is the probability of 1885,

224
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:04,920
given the rest?

225
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:07,980
And that token is
probably pretty useful.

226
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:13,960
It's basically a Q&A example,
phrased as sentence completion.

227
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,480
So it captures knowledge
about the world.

228
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:22,180
But other ones-- for example,
the first word of a sentence,

229
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:25,120
or even "founded"--

230
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:29,560
maybe not that
interesting, I think.

231
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:31,340
And perplexity doesn't care.

232
00:12:31,340 --> 00:12:38,250
It's going to charge you bits
for every bit of a deviation

233
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:41,490
from the true distribution.

234
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:44,150
So there is a bit
of a solution here,

235
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:47,190
which is you can always measure
the conditional perplexity.

236
00:12:47,190 --> 00:12:48,710
You can condition
on some prompt,

237
00:12:48,710 --> 00:12:53,530
and then you can measure the
perplexity of the remaining

238
00:12:53,530 --> 00:12:54,450
response.

239
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:56,410
So that allows you
to, in general,

240
00:12:56,410 --> 00:12:58,690
focus on certain
tokens that you believe

241
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:02,730
to be relevant
and less on others

242
00:13:02,730 --> 00:13:06,590
that are sort of incidental.

243
00:13:06,590 --> 00:13:08,916
OK.

244
00:13:08,916 --> 00:13:11,570
And also, it's
important to remember

245
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,570
that some benchmarks, even
though they are not explicitly

246
00:13:15,570 --> 00:13:17,130
a perplexity
measure, are actually

247
00:13:17,130 --> 00:13:18,930
just perplexity in disguise.

248
00:13:18,930 --> 00:13:22,110
So, for example, if you
think about closed tasks.

249
00:13:22,110 --> 00:13:26,110
There's this lambada paper
from, actually, a while ago,

250
00:13:26,110 --> 00:13:29,650
2016, which is basically
fill in the blank.

251
00:13:29,650 --> 00:13:33,290
And you have a context,
you have a sentence.

252
00:13:33,290 --> 00:13:36,380
"Do you honestly think I will
want you to have a blank."

253
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:40,260
And the goal is to
figure out that word.

254
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:44,320
And even though this is
measured in terms of accuracy,

255
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:48,900
it is really a next-token
prediction problem.

256
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:52,680
And here, it's not
perplexity of all the words,

257
00:13:52,680 --> 00:13:54,000
it's carefully chosen.

258
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,940
If you read this paper,
it's really carefully chosen

259
00:13:56,940 --> 00:14:00,060
to be words, such
that you actually need

260
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:03,780
a bunch of long-contexts
to resolve.

261
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:08,980
So this is actually something
that the early GPT papers really

262
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:14,820
latched on to because they
were interested in long-context

263
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:17,980
modeling, thinking that
that was a key to unlocking

264
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:20,900
reasoning and all these things.

265
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:24,620
And really, by
focusing on positions

266
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:29,300
where, to resolve
that, it requires

267
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:34,870
long-distance
dependencies, is a way

268
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:39,350
to sharpen the perplexity
to some phenomenon

269
00:14:39,350 --> 00:14:41,910
that you care about.

270
00:14:41,910 --> 00:14:44,270
Here's another
popular classic data

271
00:14:44,270 --> 00:14:46,890
set that you see in some
language modeling vowels.

272
00:14:46,890 --> 00:14:47,990
HellaSwag.

273
00:14:47,990 --> 00:14:49,170
So what is HellaSwag?

274
00:14:49,170 --> 00:14:52,950
They went to various different
data sets and sources,

275
00:14:52,950 --> 00:14:56,030
and came up with--
it's multiple choice,

276
00:14:56,030 --> 00:14:57,850
but it's really
sentence completion.

277
00:14:57,850 --> 00:15:00,130
And at some level,
it's perplexity.

278
00:15:00,130 --> 00:15:01,550
So here's a sentence.

279
00:15:01,550 --> 00:15:03,410
"A woman is outside
with a bucket and a dog.

280
00:15:03,410 --> 00:15:04,690
The dog is running around.

281
00:15:04,690 --> 00:15:05,805
She blank."

282
00:15:05,805 --> 00:15:07,430
And then you're trying
to fill in which

283
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:10,150
sentence completes it the best.

284
00:15:10,150 --> 00:15:12,650
So it's not exactly perplexity,
it's multiple choice.

285
00:15:12,650 --> 00:15:18,910
But in some ways,
it's perplexity.

286
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:20,970
So one word of warning.

287
00:15:20,970 --> 00:15:25,670
If you ever decide that
you're sold on perplexity

288
00:15:25,670 --> 00:15:29,070
as, really, the
measure and you're

289
00:15:29,070 --> 00:15:32,170
going to launch a leaderboard
and have people compete

290
00:15:32,170 --> 00:15:35,890
against perplexity,
just a word of caution

291
00:15:35,890 --> 00:15:39,810
is that, what is a perplexity
leaderboard look like?

292
00:15:39,810 --> 00:15:43,130
So people are going to submit
a language model to you,

293
00:15:43,130 --> 00:15:47,230
and you have to
compute a logprob.

294
00:15:47,230 --> 00:15:51,050
You send them the test data, and
they give you back a logprob.

295
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:53,130
Well, you look at
that and say, hmm,

296
00:15:53,130 --> 00:15:58,130
that might be a little
bit fishy because you

297
00:15:58,130 --> 00:16:01,650
need to trust that the
probabilities they hand back

298
00:16:01,650 --> 00:16:06,390
to you are valid and
that they sum to 1.

299
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:08,490
Otherwise, I could
just have implement LM,

300
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:14,410
and it always returns 1
or, I guess, logprob 0.

301
00:16:14,410 --> 00:16:17,270
And I would get very
good perplexity,

302
00:16:17,270 --> 00:16:22,530
but clearly, that's not
a valid distribution.

303
00:16:22,530 --> 00:16:24,210
And so with this
contract it's not

304
00:16:24,210 --> 00:16:27,650
really easy to check
that someone is actually

305
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:29,510
internally having
a log probability,

306
00:16:29,510 --> 00:16:30,520
so there's some trust.

307
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:33,860
Maybe you have to look
at the code or something.

308
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:39,060
For downstream tasks, this
is much more straightforward

309
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:40,707
because you can
have a black box LM.

310
00:16:40,707 --> 00:16:42,540
I give you a prompt,
you give me a response,

311
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:46,260
and I can just use that response
and calculate some accuracy.

312
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:48,140
So perplexity is
fundamentally different

313
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,080
because it deals
with distributions,

314
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:52,660
and you have to
be a bit careful.

315
00:16:52,660 --> 00:16:55,980
And not to mention,
there's lots of problems

316
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:57,200
with autoregressive models.

317
00:16:57,200 --> 00:17:01,180
But if you have VAEs or there's
some models where you can't even

318
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:04,780
compute the distribution--
you can compute only a bound--

319
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:08,460
then you have to really actually
trust the math as well that this

320
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:11,060
is actually a valid bound.

321
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:12,560
OK.

322
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:16,420
So The summary of perplexity
is that it is actually

323
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:22,819
still used quite heavily in
language model development.

324
00:17:22,819 --> 00:17:24,280
It has some nice properties.

325
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:27,619
We saw that it's generally
used for scaling laws

326
00:17:27,619 --> 00:17:30,910
because it's also
smoothly varying

327
00:17:30,910 --> 00:17:35,510
with scale, which is important
for getting scaling laws.

328
00:17:35,510 --> 00:17:40,190
But, at the end of the day,
especially if you're not

329
00:17:40,190 --> 00:17:45,070
a believer, then you need some
benchmarks to really capture

330
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:46,990
real world situations,
to convince people

331
00:17:46,990 --> 00:17:48,990
that your language
model is good.

332
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:53,090
For the believers, if I show you
a remarkably slow perplexity,

333
00:17:53,090 --> 00:17:56,270
they'll be convinced.

334
00:17:56,270 --> 00:17:58,530
OK, any questions
about perplexity?

335
00:17:58,530 --> 00:18:10,370

336
00:18:10,370 --> 00:18:10,870
OK.

337
00:18:10,870 --> 00:18:14,550
Hopefully, everyone's
a believer now.

338
00:18:14,550 --> 00:18:18,590
OK, so let's talk
about exam benchmarks.

339
00:18:18,590 --> 00:18:21,830
So exams, as all of
your students know,

340
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:26,130
is a way that we
use to test humans.

341
00:18:26,130 --> 00:18:28,330
And so you can adopt
the same mentality

342
00:18:28,330 --> 00:18:30,730
to test language models.

343
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:32,370
The nice thing about
exams is that you

344
00:18:32,370 --> 00:18:35,570
have a very careful
control over the subject,

345
00:18:35,570 --> 00:18:38,410
over the difficulty.

346
00:18:38,410 --> 00:18:42,510
You can design them to have
unambiguous correct answers,

347
00:18:42,510 --> 00:18:44,650
which makes them easy to grade.

348
00:18:44,650 --> 00:18:47,930
So there's a lot of nice
properties about exams.

349
00:18:47,930 --> 00:18:52,410
And so, for this reason, a
lot of LM benchmarking culture

350
00:18:52,410 --> 00:18:56,210
was based on just
this idea of exams.

351
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:59,783
So obviously, there's
been a lot of benchmarks,

352
00:18:59,783 --> 00:19:01,450
but I'll just highlight
some of the ones

353
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:04,850
that I think have
been influential

354
00:19:04,850 --> 00:19:07,290
in the development
of language models.

355
00:19:07,290 --> 00:19:11,450
So this one is MMLU, or Massive
Multitask Language Understanding

356
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:15,770
from Hendrycks et al, 2020.

357
00:19:15,770 --> 00:19:19,010
And at that time,
it wasn't clear

358
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:23,690
that language models were
really like general purpose task

359
00:19:23,690 --> 00:19:24,190
solvers.

360
00:19:24,190 --> 00:19:29,460
This was around the time
when GPT-3 came out.

361
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:31,780
Many people, I think, still
thought of language models

362
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:33,800
as, well, language models.

363
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:36,340
You generate fluent English.

364
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:38,940
But they had this
idea that, well,

365
00:19:38,940 --> 00:19:41,880
let's just try to be
ahead of the curve here.

366
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:46,040
We're going to go
through 57 subjects,

367
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:50,140
we're going to have
a team of students

368
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:52,900
scour the internet
for different sources,

369
00:19:52,900 --> 00:20:00,020
and really put together this
very comprehensive data set.

370
00:20:00,020 --> 00:20:03,420
And despite the name MMLU,
which has the word Language

371
00:20:03,420 --> 00:20:05,180
Understanding, it's
really about testing

372
00:20:05,180 --> 00:20:07,580
knowledge and reasoning
and less so just

373
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:09,660
pure language understanding.

374
00:20:09,660 --> 00:20:14,020
And the way they evaluated
it was with GPT-3,

375
00:20:14,020 --> 00:20:15,720
using few-shot prompting.

376
00:20:15,720 --> 00:20:22,140
So they essentially said,
I have formed this prompt.

377
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,890
The following are questions
about high school mathematics.

378
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:28,390
Some in-context examples of
question, answer, question,

379
00:20:28,390 --> 00:20:31,510
answer, and then the final
question, and then the model

380
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:33,830
has to predict the answer.

381
00:20:33,830 --> 00:20:35,650
So this seems very mundane.

382
00:20:35,650 --> 00:20:38,070
But at that time
it was, I think,

383
00:20:38,070 --> 00:20:41,630
pretty radical that
you would construct

384
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:46,270
this fairly complicated question
and with in-context examples

385
00:20:46,270 --> 00:20:48,070
and everything, and
expect a language

386
00:20:48,070 --> 00:20:51,830
model to actually do
something reasonable.

387
00:20:51,830 --> 00:20:57,970
And sure enough, at the outset,
the models were not great,

388
00:20:57,970 --> 00:20:58,550
I would say.

389
00:20:58,550 --> 00:21:01,430

390
00:21:01,430 --> 00:21:04,890
Some of the small models
were barely above chance.

391
00:21:04,890 --> 00:21:07,470
And the larger
model-- this GPT-3--

392
00:21:07,470 --> 00:21:13,910
was actually well above chance,
so this was kind of encouraging.

393
00:21:13,910 --> 00:21:21,930
And then over the
years, as we know,

394
00:21:21,930 --> 00:21:26,030
this benchmark has
essentially saturated.

395
00:21:26,030 --> 00:21:29,550
So this is a site I don't think
this has all the data in it,

396
00:21:29,550 --> 00:21:32,110
but about 3.5 Turbo.

397
00:21:32,110 --> 00:21:35,290
This was like--

398
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:39,570
I guess, beginning of
2024-- oh, '23 actually--

399
00:21:39,570 --> 00:21:41,090
to GPT-4.

400
00:21:41,090 --> 00:21:48,030
And then, now it's in the 90s.

401
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:52,250
So there's also a
link you can follow up

402
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:54,210
later on, where you can
actually browse some

403
00:21:54,210 --> 00:21:57,610
of the predictions of these
models, which is always

404
00:21:57,610 --> 00:22:00,690
fun to see what models
actually goof up on

405
00:22:00,690 --> 00:22:03,850
and what they get right.

406
00:22:03,850 --> 00:22:06,355
OK.

407
00:22:06,355 --> 00:22:10,930
So around 2024, when it
seemed like MMLU, there

408
00:22:10,930 --> 00:22:13,757
was a lot of progress
made, it was like, OK--

409
00:22:13,757 --> 00:22:15,340
you're going to see
this theme again--

410
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:18,570
OK, this benchmark
is getting too easy.

411
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:22,525
Let's try to make it harder.

412
00:22:22,525 --> 00:22:23,900
Well, first of
all, they actually

413
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:26,320
noticed that there was some
noisy and trivial problems,

414
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:27,900
so let's get rid of them.

415
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:30,960
Four choices might be
a little bit too easy,

416
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:34,300
so let's expand that to 10.

417
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:38,820
At that point, it was much more
in vogue to use chain of thought

418
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:42,740
to solve these benchmarks,
whereas when GPT-3 came out,

419
00:22:42,740 --> 00:22:46,300
that wasn't really a thing.

420
00:22:46,300 --> 00:22:48,820
And it's clear that
some of these questions

421
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:52,060
could benefit from a
few reasoning steps.

422
00:22:52,060 --> 00:22:55,740
So this new
benchmark was harder.

423
00:22:55,740 --> 00:22:59,820
So now the accuracy of
the model is back at 33.

424
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:04,140
But as you can maybe
see from the progress

425
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:07,600
so far it started low.

426
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:13,200
And now it's back to I guess
it's like 88 almost 90, OK.

427
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:18,350
So this benchmark might be a
little bit too easy as well.

428
00:23:18,350 --> 00:23:20,830
OK, so there's another
benchmark called

429
00:23:20,830 --> 00:23:26,790
GPQA, which stands
for Google-Proof QA.

430
00:23:26,790 --> 00:23:31,950
And the idea here is
that OK, how can we

431
00:23:31,950 --> 00:23:35,930
get a really good benchmark?

432
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:38,270
And the idea is that, well,
if you can solve a problem

433
00:23:38,270 --> 00:23:39,710
by looking at Google.

434
00:23:39,710 --> 00:23:42,230
Google has the internet
and a language model

435
00:23:42,230 --> 00:23:43,370
is trained on the internet.

436
00:23:43,370 --> 00:23:46,410
So if you can just solve it
then that's probably too easy.

437
00:23:46,410 --> 00:23:49,970
So they got some now PhD level.

438
00:23:49,970 --> 00:23:55,910
So I think MMLU was
think about as actually--

439
00:23:55,910 --> 00:24:00,050
maybe some of these were like
undergrad but maybe grad level.

440
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:03,110
But now, it's more explicitly
about questions that

441
00:24:03,110 --> 00:24:06,310
are meant to be pretty hard.

442
00:24:06,310 --> 00:24:08,897
And at this point
now you say, OK,

443
00:24:08,897 --> 00:24:10,730
how do I come up with
really hard questions?

444
00:24:10,730 --> 00:24:13,790
You actually have to go through
quite a bit of human labor

445
00:24:13,790 --> 00:24:15,960
to get really hard questions.

446
00:24:15,960 --> 00:24:19,920
So this was 61 PhD
contractors from Upwork.

447
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:21,680
Someone writes a question.

448
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,680
It goes through an expert
validation which then provides

449
00:24:24,680 --> 00:24:26,920
feedback on the questions.

450
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:29,740
And then the question writer
goes and revises the question.

451
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:32,360
And another expert
looks at the question.

452
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:36,340
And then you have the question.

453
00:24:36,340 --> 00:24:38,360
And then you send it
to some non-experts

454
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:41,220
who are given Google access.

455
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:44,120
And then you see if they
can solve the question.

456
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:47,320
So there's a subset of these
questions called the DIAMOND set

457
00:24:47,320 --> 00:24:51,800
that you might see in
people's leaderboards

458
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:56,000
where if the two experts agree
and at one of the non-experts

459
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:59,280
is able to answer the question,
then that's deemed a good kind

460
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:01,240
of DIAMOND question.

461
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:05,160
So this is pretty
rigorous review process.

462
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:12,300
And PhD experts were
only able to get 65%.

463
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:15,680
So this is I think mostly
four-way multiple choice.

464
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:18,280
So this is above random.

465
00:25:18,280 --> 00:25:25,260
But this is not a good grade if
you're getting 65% on an exam.

466
00:25:25,260 --> 00:25:28,900
And it's not experts, even if
they had 30 minutes with access

467
00:25:28,900 --> 00:25:33,860
to Google, they were getting a
bit over chance, but not much.

468
00:25:33,860 --> 00:25:37,820
And at that point
GPT-4 was like 39%,

469
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:40,960
and let's see how
it's doing now.

470
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:45,140

471
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:54,000
So GPQA is now again 94.

472
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:01,692
So these benchmarks have a shelf
life of not too high I guess.

473
00:26:01,692 --> 00:26:03,400
I will say that some
of these benchmarks,

474
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:05,520
even though they saturate
the larger models,

475
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:07,060
they're still useful
for developing

476
00:26:07,060 --> 00:26:10,940
smaller models or fitting
scaling laws and so on.

477
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:14,950
So then, yeah.

478
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:18,670
How do you know that
when these tests are

479
00:26:18,670 --> 00:26:22,390
being used for training
and actually your models

480
00:26:22,390 --> 00:26:24,270
are communicating with these?

481
00:26:24,270 --> 00:26:26,690
Yeah, so this is a very
important question.

482
00:26:26,690 --> 00:26:29,750
So how do you know that
these questions aren't

483
00:26:29,750 --> 00:26:32,390
just in the training set?

484
00:26:32,390 --> 00:26:35,270
So the short answer is we
don't know because I don't know

485
00:26:35,270 --> 00:26:37,150
what's in the training set.

486
00:26:37,150 --> 00:26:39,030
I'll come back to
this contamination

487
00:26:39,030 --> 00:26:42,530
point a little bit later
and ways of addressing it.

488
00:26:42,530 --> 00:26:44,110
But you should always--

489
00:26:44,110 --> 00:26:45,330
I'm glad you asked this.

490
00:26:45,330 --> 00:26:46,990
You should always
take these numbers

491
00:26:46,990 --> 00:26:51,170
with a little bit of a grain of
salt, because it's only trust.

492
00:26:51,170 --> 00:26:54,590

493
00:26:54,590 --> 00:26:55,970
Maybe just to say one more.

494
00:26:55,970 --> 00:26:59,870
I think train contamination
is a little bit subtle.

495
00:26:59,870 --> 00:27:01,830
Because it's probably
not the case that

496
00:27:01,830 --> 00:27:04,310
they literally train on the set.

497
00:27:04,310 --> 00:27:08,050
But questions can be
derived from other sources.

498
00:27:08,050 --> 00:27:09,760
And those sources
could be trained on.

499
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:14,720
So often contamination is
a much more subtle process

500
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:19,440
than literally accidentally
training on the test.

501
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:20,700
Was there another question?

502
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:21,480
Same question.

503
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:23,280
OK, great.

504
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:25,800
Good always good
to be skeptical.

505
00:27:25,800 --> 00:27:29,320
OK, so the final exam
benchmark I'll talk about

506
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:34,400
is something ominously
called Humanity's Last Exam.

507
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:36,480
This is from last year.

508
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:38,320
And they were saying,
OK, well these models

509
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:39,500
are getting so good.

510
00:27:39,500 --> 00:27:41,560
Let's just give it
all we can to create

511
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:44,080
something that's really tough.

512
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:46,460
So this is multimodal,
many subjects.

513
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:47,620
It's still multiple choice.

514
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:50,200
And I guess some short answer.

515
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:53,340
So this was crowdsourced.

516
00:27:53,340 --> 00:27:59,120
They had some money to
incentivize people who were

517
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:01,460
into money to create questions.

518
00:28:01,460 --> 00:28:03,240
They also incentivize
with authorship

519
00:28:03,240 --> 00:28:06,160
for those who are into that.

520
00:28:06,160 --> 00:28:08,740
And they went through
multiple stages of reviews

521
00:28:08,740 --> 00:28:10,860
filtered by frontier models.

522
00:28:10,860 --> 00:28:12,540
There's a whole process.

523
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:15,780
Another answer to your question
is that they also held out

524
00:28:15,780 --> 00:28:18,480
a private set that was
not released publicly,

525
00:28:18,480 --> 00:28:22,340
so that they could make
sure that this did not

526
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:24,260
enter training.

527
00:28:24,260 --> 00:28:26,220
And then you just
hope that the APIs

528
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:29,440
that you-- because you still
have to send this to the model,

529
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:32,740
that those prompts don't
end up in the training.

530
00:28:32,740 --> 00:28:36,740
But let's hope that's the case.

531
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:41,640
So every data set
paper looks like this.

532
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:44,640
Previous benchmarks, look
the models are doing so well.

533
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:48,460
My data set, look all the
models are doing really poorly.

534
00:28:48,460 --> 00:28:50,320
So this is a common pattern.

535
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:52,200
And this was especially
single digits.

536
00:28:52,200 --> 00:28:55,620
It's pretty abysmal here.

537
00:28:55,620 --> 00:29:01,020
And let's see how
we're doing in 2026.

538
00:29:01,020 --> 00:29:05,680
So this one is-- it
still has some room.

539
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:07,150
So it's only at--

540
00:29:07,150 --> 00:29:11,670
even Mythos is only at 64.7.

541
00:29:11,670 --> 00:29:16,050
So this is indeed a pretty-- it
seems like a pretty hard exam.

542
00:29:16,050 --> 00:29:20,550

543
00:29:20,550 --> 00:29:26,230
OK, so to summarize this
section, as you can see,

544
00:29:26,230 --> 00:29:28,470
we've trained it towards
harder and harder questions

545
00:29:28,470 --> 00:29:33,030
as the models improve and we
saturate existing benchmarks.

546
00:29:33,030 --> 00:29:37,230
It's interesting that multiple
choice format still survives

547
00:29:37,230 --> 00:29:40,095
because it's--

548
00:29:40,095 --> 00:29:41,470
with multiple
choice, I mean, you

549
00:29:41,470 --> 00:29:44,030
can make it as
difficult as you want.

550
00:29:44,030 --> 00:29:47,130
So some people think, well,
multiple choice is too easy.

551
00:29:47,130 --> 00:29:48,990
Well, I mean, I can
give you a very hard

552
00:29:48,990 --> 00:29:51,010
multiple-choice question.

553
00:29:51,010 --> 00:29:53,630
That's not really the point.

554
00:29:53,630 --> 00:29:56,670
I think more the point is
that multiple choice restricts

555
00:29:56,670 --> 00:29:58,790
the set of questions
you can ask.

556
00:29:58,790 --> 00:30:03,510
But in terms of difficulty, no
problem with multiple choice.

557
00:30:03,510 --> 00:30:07,880
The main problem with
exam-based questions

558
00:30:07,880 --> 00:30:12,400
is that this does not
capture real-world usage.

559
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:17,160
No one asks HLE questions
to a language model

560
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:20,440
except for when you're
evaluating on HLE.

561
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:24,160
Most of the time people are
asking open-ended questions.

562
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:26,620
They don't even necessarily
have a correct answer.

563
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:29,800
They might not be well
formed, and these benchmarks

564
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:33,080
do not capture that.

565
00:30:33,080 --> 00:30:37,077
OK, so question.

566
00:30:37,077 --> 00:30:39,660
How is the model output compared
with the ground truth answer?

567
00:30:39,660 --> 00:30:42,520
So for HLE?

568
00:30:42,520 --> 00:30:44,360
For accuracy, how is
the model's output

569
00:30:44,360 --> 00:30:47,000
compared with the
ground truth answer,

570
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:48,400
to calculate the accuracy?

571
00:30:48,400 --> 00:30:51,880
To calculate the accuracy
for how is the ground truth

572
00:30:51,880 --> 00:30:52,800
compared.

573
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:56,640
So for multiple
choice, it's just like,

574
00:30:56,640 --> 00:30:59,078
did you get the right answer?

575
00:30:59,078 --> 00:31:01,620
So will we check the probability
distribution for each option

576
00:31:01,620 --> 00:31:04,850
or how is--

577
00:31:04,850 --> 00:31:09,330
So the language
model generates C.

578
00:31:09,330 --> 00:31:12,850
And my answer is either
B or C or A or D,

579
00:31:12,850 --> 00:31:16,010
and I just check if
they're the same or not.

580
00:31:16,010 --> 00:31:18,890
So we are only
generating one token?

581
00:31:18,890 --> 00:31:19,990
Yes, well, OK.

582
00:31:19,990 --> 00:31:22,150
So then the question is
less about evaluation.

583
00:31:22,150 --> 00:31:25,250
But how does the model
generate an answer?

584
00:31:25,250 --> 00:31:27,710
So there's a few options.

585
00:31:27,710 --> 00:31:30,010
One is that you generate--

586
00:31:30,010 --> 00:31:32,450
literally you sample and
it generates a letter.

587
00:31:32,450 --> 00:31:35,670
Or more commonly now you
generate a chain of thought.

588
00:31:35,670 --> 00:31:38,450
You generate the answer
maybe some explanation.

589
00:31:38,450 --> 00:31:43,690
But there is a way of extracting
an answer which does matter.

590
00:31:43,690 --> 00:31:47,150
And language model evaluations
can be very sensitive to that

591
00:31:47,150 --> 00:31:49,230
but I'm not going to
talk about that here.

592
00:31:49,230 --> 00:31:52,370

593
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:56,890
So let's talk about a very
different class of benchmarks

594
00:31:56,890 --> 00:32:00,490
called chat benchmarks.

595
00:32:00,490 --> 00:32:03,850
So far we've talked about
multiple-choice tasks.

596
00:32:03,850 --> 00:32:07,190
So they're still pretty good
at capturing some notion

597
00:32:07,190 --> 00:32:09,950
of intelligence and difficulty.

598
00:32:09,950 --> 00:32:13,630
But most people don't ask a
multiple-choice exam question

599
00:32:13,630 --> 00:32:15,990
to their AI assistant
unless they're

600
00:32:15,990 --> 00:32:19,530
trying to use their AI system
to do their exam for them,

601
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:21,270
which you would never.

602
00:32:21,270 --> 00:32:23,150
One does that.

603
00:32:23,150 --> 00:32:29,150
OK, so here's an example
semi-inspired by a real world

604
00:32:29,150 --> 00:32:30,110
use case.

605
00:32:30,110 --> 00:32:33,650
So I might say I want to make
a beet salad with cheese.

606
00:32:33,650 --> 00:32:36,510
What herbs would work well
and what wouldn't work well?

607
00:32:36,510 --> 00:32:39,170
OK, so this is a very
open-ended question.

608
00:32:39,170 --> 00:32:42,810
And then you get some
response, which is, again,

609
00:32:42,810 --> 00:32:45,350
an open-ended response.

610
00:32:45,350 --> 00:32:48,710
And then the question is, how
do you evaluate this thing?

611
00:32:48,710 --> 00:32:51,370
You can't just check
exact match with equality.

612
00:32:51,370 --> 00:32:54,310
There's no ground truth even.

613
00:32:54,310 --> 00:32:56,670
So there's a few ideas here.

614
00:32:56,670 --> 00:32:58,390
We can go through them.

615
00:32:58,390 --> 00:33:04,840
So one of the ideas was
pioneered by this Chatbot Arena.

616
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:09,640
And the idea is, well,
you ask humans, OK.

617
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:13,400
But the way they ask humans,
I think is a clever idea.

618
00:33:13,400 --> 00:33:18,000
So they basically had
this set of this website

619
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:23,680
where any random person from
the internet can go in and chat.

620
00:33:23,680 --> 00:33:27,080
And then instead of
a normal as assistant

621
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:29,440
in getting one
response, you actually

622
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:33,680
get two responses from
two different models

623
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:38,120
which are anonymized, and then
they rate which one is better.

624
00:33:38,120 --> 00:33:41,000
So for example, you
put in this response,

625
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:45,760
you get these two, assistant A,
assistant B. And then you ask,

626
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:49,920
A is better, both are good,
both are bad, B is better?

627
00:33:49,920 --> 00:33:53,840
So using this
process, you can get

628
00:33:53,840 --> 00:33:58,930
a bunch of pairwise comparison
data of model A, model B, which

629
00:33:58,930 --> 00:34:00,330
one is better?

630
00:34:00,330 --> 00:34:03,730
And then you can use this
to compute ELO rankings

631
00:34:03,730 --> 00:34:04,930
based on this.

632
00:34:04,930 --> 00:34:07,890
So I don't know how many of
you are familiar with this

633
00:34:07,890 --> 00:34:11,690
but just get everyone
on the same page.

634
00:34:11,690 --> 00:34:17,489
In these ELO rankings, you
define your rating model

635
00:34:17,489 --> 00:34:20,610
as a probability of a
particular model being

636
00:34:20,610 --> 00:34:27,090
another [INAUDIBLE] model
as some function of the ELO

637
00:34:27,090 --> 00:34:34,889
scores of model B and model A.
So the larger model ELO score

638
00:34:34,889 --> 00:34:37,590
of A is the higher
the probability.

639
00:34:37,590 --> 00:34:39,290
So it's a smooth mapping.

640
00:34:39,290 --> 00:34:42,909
And then you fit this
model with the parameters,

641
00:34:42,909 --> 00:34:44,850
which are the ELO
ratings to maximize

642
00:34:44,850 --> 00:34:48,350
the probability of the
pairwise comparisons, OK.

643
00:34:48,350 --> 00:34:49,929
And then as a
result, you basically

644
00:34:49,929 --> 00:34:55,949
get a rating for
each of your models.

645
00:34:55,949 --> 00:34:59,510
OK, so this is what Chatbot
Arena nowadays known

646
00:34:59,510 --> 00:35:02,230
as Arena AI did.

647
00:35:02,230 --> 00:35:06,750
And you can get
this nice ranking.

648
00:35:06,750 --> 00:35:10,950
I guess Claude Opus is
doing pretty well here

649
00:35:10,950 --> 00:35:14,710
but some other models
are on the list as well.

650
00:35:14,710 --> 00:35:15,950
OK.

651
00:35:15,950 --> 00:35:20,010
So there's a few things that
are nice about this setup.

652
00:35:20,010 --> 00:35:24,310
One is that you have
real-world prompts.

653
00:35:24,310 --> 00:35:27,148
So the idea is that anyone
can come to the site,

654
00:35:27,148 --> 00:35:29,190
and the incentive for
someone to come to the site

655
00:35:29,190 --> 00:35:32,430
is that they get free
access to a language model.

656
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:35,310
And so there's this
implicit assumption

657
00:35:35,310 --> 00:35:39,330
that they're actually trying to
use it to do something useful.

658
00:35:39,330 --> 00:35:47,630
And therefore you get
a real-world prompt.

659
00:35:47,630 --> 00:35:52,110
But one thing you should keep in
mind is, who are these people?

660
00:35:52,110 --> 00:35:54,240
Random person on
the internet is--

661
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:56,860
I don't know what
that distribution.

662
00:35:56,860 --> 00:35:59,120
Random person on the internet
who comes to La Marina.

663
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:01,460
I don't know what that
distribution looks like.

664
00:36:01,460 --> 00:36:03,900
I think in their paper,
they have some demographics,

665
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:07,320
but demographics don't
tell the whole story.

666
00:36:07,320 --> 00:36:09,560
You could worry about
different biases coming

667
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:12,820
from the different models.

668
00:36:12,820 --> 00:36:13,860
Maybe spammers.

669
00:36:13,860 --> 00:36:19,520
People are trying to game, who
maybe even submitted a model

670
00:36:19,520 --> 00:36:24,040
and they want their
model to look good.

671
00:36:24,040 --> 00:36:25,460
You just don't know.

672
00:36:25,460 --> 00:36:29,080
It's a little bit
of a Wild West.

673
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:32,760
There's another issue which
is that the binary preference,

674
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:35,880
which is better, A or B
is very nice and simple

675
00:36:35,880 --> 00:36:41,160
and feeds into this rating,
but does conflate style

676
00:36:41,160 --> 00:36:43,160
and correctness.

677
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:45,100
So ELO ratings for chess
makes a lot of sense,

678
00:36:45,100 --> 00:36:47,360
because the only thing that
matters is, did you win?

679
00:36:47,360 --> 00:36:54,810
But which one is better
is much less clear cut.

680
00:36:54,810 --> 00:37:00,310
And there's questions of
the human is the judge here.

681
00:37:00,310 --> 00:37:02,050
The person who
puts in the prompt

682
00:37:02,050 --> 00:37:03,990
is answering the question.

683
00:37:03,990 --> 00:37:06,230
And this is good
because in some level,

684
00:37:06,230 --> 00:37:10,810
because the person
has some intent

685
00:37:10,810 --> 00:37:15,330
and they can declare whether
their intent was met.

686
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:16,938
To some extent,
except for they're

687
00:37:16,938 --> 00:37:18,730
presumably asking the
question because they

688
00:37:18,730 --> 00:37:19,670
don't know the answer.

689
00:37:19,670 --> 00:37:22,010
So if I give you
two answers, how

690
00:37:22,010 --> 00:37:25,090
are they to judge
which one is correct?

691
00:37:25,090 --> 00:37:27,630
And then there's also the
problem of sycophancy,

692
00:37:27,630 --> 00:37:31,210
where maybe more
pleasing answers

693
00:37:31,210 --> 00:37:34,570
are going to be
upweighted highly compared

694
00:37:34,570 --> 00:37:37,770
to correct but honest answers.

695
00:37:37,770 --> 00:37:42,810
So there's a lot of
potential issues here.

696
00:37:42,810 --> 00:37:45,370
But the nice thing
about this method, which

697
00:37:45,370 --> 00:37:47,690
is that you don't
need to feed the same

698
00:37:47,690 --> 00:37:50,755
prompts to all the models,
and this is the beauty of ELO.

699
00:37:50,755 --> 00:37:52,130
In chess, you
don't have to play.

700
00:37:52,130 --> 00:37:54,430
Everyone plays everyone else.

701
00:37:54,430 --> 00:37:57,050
It can just be some
sparse sample of that.

702
00:37:57,050 --> 00:37:59,230
And you can still
derive rankings as long

703
00:37:59,230 --> 00:38:02,270
as the graph is connected.

704
00:38:02,270 --> 00:38:04,550
And this is important
because human is rating

705
00:38:04,550 --> 00:38:06,950
and you can't expect a
human to rate all the models

706
00:38:06,950 --> 00:38:10,310
or even more than two models.

707
00:38:10,310 --> 00:38:12,430
And the nice thing
as well is that

708
00:38:12,430 --> 00:38:14,450
as new prompts and
new models come in,

709
00:38:14,450 --> 00:38:21,030
there's a natural story for
making this updating over time.

710
00:38:21,030 --> 00:38:26,790
So let me talk about
a different approach.

711
00:38:26,790 --> 00:38:30,710
So this is AlpacaEval from 2023.

712
00:38:30,710 --> 00:38:37,270
There were some instructions
derived from various sources.

713
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:44,130
And the metric here is the win
rate against a baseline model.

714
00:38:44,130 --> 00:38:47,030
So you have a model,
AlpacaEval says,

715
00:38:47,030 --> 00:38:49,160
I'm going to generate
it for a prompt,

716
00:38:49,160 --> 00:38:52,240
generate using let's
say baseline model GPT-4

717
00:38:52,240 --> 00:38:57,640
preview at that time and then
judge using GPT preview, which

718
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:59,000
one is better?

719
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:03,960
So immediately, this raises
a question of potential bias.

720
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:06,920
But let's put that
aside for now.

721
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:09,160
So this is basically
an instantiation

722
00:39:09,160 --> 00:39:12,920
of LLM as a judge, which
is very popular these days

723
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:16,200
for evaluating language models.

724
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:18,120
And you can mitigate
this bias by having

725
00:39:18,120 --> 00:39:21,720
multiple judges and ensembling
and so on and so forth.

726
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:24,040
So one problem with
initial AlpacaEval

727
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:29,040
is that LLM judges
favor long responses.

728
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:31,400
And this led to a bunch
of leaderboard gaming

729
00:39:31,400 --> 00:39:34,800
where a bunch of models that
were fine tuned were submitted

730
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:38,520
and they got really
high LLM AlpacaEval

731
00:39:38,520 --> 00:39:41,300
performance just because
the responses were longer.

732
00:39:41,300 --> 00:39:45,560
So this got fixed in a
subsequent paper where

733
00:39:45,560 --> 00:39:51,490
the use a very simple regression
method to de-bias the metric.

734
00:39:51,490 --> 00:39:57,370
OK, but this raises a more
general question here,

735
00:39:57,370 --> 00:40:00,610
which is not necessarily
specific to AlpacaEval which is,

736
00:40:00,610 --> 00:40:02,890
how do you evaluate a metric?

737
00:40:02,890 --> 00:40:06,630
So we've been talking
about evaluation of models.

738
00:40:06,630 --> 00:40:09,390
And if I have a metric, how
you can evaluate a model.

739
00:40:09,390 --> 00:40:11,450
But if I'm coming
up with a metric,

740
00:40:11,450 --> 00:40:14,370
how do this metric is any good?

741
00:40:14,370 --> 00:40:17,030
So this is a hard problem.

742
00:40:17,030 --> 00:40:20,890
And there's no answer here.

743
00:40:20,890 --> 00:40:24,730
One thing you can do is
more of a sanity check

744
00:40:24,730 --> 00:40:27,210
is you can look at
correlation with other metrics

745
00:40:27,210 --> 00:40:33,810
with the idea that higher isn't
necessarily better in all cases

746
00:40:33,810 --> 00:40:36,190
unless you are trying to
mimic this other metric.

747
00:40:36,190 --> 00:40:41,610
So AlpacaEval correlation
with Chatbot Arena

748
00:40:41,610 --> 00:40:46,260
is 0.98 which is quite high.

749
00:40:46,260 --> 00:40:50,820
So which suggests
that you could--

750
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:56,800
let's say you wanted to
evaluate on a Chatbot Arena,

751
00:40:56,800 --> 00:40:59,620
but you're too shy
to put your model

752
00:40:59,620 --> 00:41:01,880
or you don't want to wait
for humans or whatever,

753
00:41:01,880 --> 00:41:07,980
you can use AlpacaEval
to evaluate instead.

754
00:41:07,980 --> 00:41:09,520
Now, this correlation,
of course,

755
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:13,700
is with respect to a
certain set of models.

756
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:17,220
And so this might
not hold for models

757
00:41:17,220 --> 00:41:22,860
that let's say are stronger
than GPT-4 preview.

758
00:41:22,860 --> 00:41:25,620
So at this point, this
leaderboard I don't think

759
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:27,940
is really--

760
00:41:27,940 --> 00:41:31,220
hasn't been maintained
in over a year but this

761
00:41:31,220 --> 00:41:34,940
is what it looked
like at that time.

762
00:41:34,940 --> 00:41:39,240
So there's another
benchmark I'll talk about,

763
00:41:39,240 --> 00:41:45,640
which is WildBench and this one
was sourced a bunch of examples

764
00:41:45,640 --> 00:41:48,700
from human-chatbot
conversations.

765
00:41:48,700 --> 00:41:53,400
So like Chatbot Arena, they
also put up a free service

766
00:41:53,400 --> 00:41:54,960
where people could
chat with this bot

767
00:41:54,960 --> 00:42:00,040
and they collected the queries.

768
00:42:00,040 --> 00:42:09,400
And then like AlpacaEval,
they used LLM as a judge

769
00:42:09,400 --> 00:42:14,480
to evaluate the model.

770
00:42:14,480 --> 00:42:17,760
The main innovation
here is the use

771
00:42:17,760 --> 00:42:21,600
of a checklist,
which is generated

772
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:26,680
and specific to a
particular prompt or task.

773
00:42:26,680 --> 00:42:28,880
And the idea here
is that if you just

774
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:34,760
ask a language model to judge
whether this response is good

775
00:42:34,760 --> 00:42:35,880
or not.

776
00:42:35,880 --> 00:42:39,400
This is in some sense a
very ill-defined task.

777
00:42:39,400 --> 00:42:41,970
I don't know what depends
on what you care about.

778
00:42:41,970 --> 00:42:44,850
And so the idea here is that
by having a checklist or some

779
00:42:44,850 --> 00:42:50,250
of rubric, this greatly scopes
and makes the task evaluation

780
00:42:50,250 --> 00:42:53,530
task more well-defined.

781
00:42:53,530 --> 00:42:57,250
So they did this, also,
they showed correlation

782
00:42:57,250 --> 00:43:00,410
with Chatbot Arena
which admittedly

783
00:43:00,410 --> 00:43:04,290
is a little bit circular because
then you can ask why, well,

784
00:43:04,290 --> 00:43:06,850
is Chatbot Arena really
the ground truth?

785
00:43:06,850 --> 00:43:11,410
But at least we're all in
the same boat together.

786
00:43:11,410 --> 00:43:16,050
OK, so to conclude this
section, how do you

787
00:43:16,050 --> 00:43:20,570
evaluate open-ended responses?

788
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:24,730
There's no clear solution
here but there's some ideas.

789
00:43:24,730 --> 00:43:31,930
Pairwise comparisons between a
reference and a model response

790
00:43:31,930 --> 00:43:35,370
is generally a good
idea because especially

791
00:43:35,370 --> 00:43:37,370
for very similar
responses, you can

792
00:43:37,370 --> 00:43:38,888
give directional information.

793
00:43:38,888 --> 00:43:40,680
Well, this one's slightly
better than this.

794
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:43,900
But in terms of absolute ranking
like is this a 7 out of 10

795
00:43:43,900 --> 00:43:45,160
or a 8 out of 10?

796
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:48,980
That tends to be a
lot less high signal.

797
00:43:48,980 --> 00:43:51,300
One should always
be aware of biases

798
00:43:51,300 --> 00:43:55,580
both in terms of
humans or LLM judges.

799
00:43:55,580 --> 00:43:58,860
They have different biases,
but nonetheless that's

800
00:43:58,860 --> 00:44:03,180
something important
to pay attention to.

801
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:05,780
Perhaps the only thing
you might be able to do

802
00:44:05,780 --> 00:44:10,380
is you evaluate over
multiple judges, humans,

803
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:13,180
and judges of all
everything is saying

804
00:44:13,180 --> 00:44:15,020
your model is better than--

805
00:44:15,020 --> 00:44:17,220
maybe your model is better.

806
00:44:17,220 --> 00:44:21,300
And finally, the
evaluation problem here

807
00:44:21,300 --> 00:44:23,300
of open-ended response.

808
00:44:23,300 --> 00:44:26,700
I would emphasize is
not really well-defined.

809
00:44:26,700 --> 00:44:29,460
And so increasingly,
I think it's

810
00:44:29,460 --> 00:44:34,980
important to define rubrics
or checklists to improve

811
00:44:34,980 --> 00:44:40,640
the reliability or
well-definedness

812
00:44:40,640 --> 00:44:42,460
of an actual evaluation.

813
00:44:42,460 --> 00:44:44,980
And this is true regardless
of a human and a judge.

814
00:44:44,980 --> 00:44:46,680
So anyone who's
done crowdsourcing

815
00:44:46,680 --> 00:44:49,440
knows that if you just
ask a human to rate

816
00:44:49,440 --> 00:44:51,700
something without
giving some of rubric,

817
00:44:51,700 --> 00:44:55,340
you're probably going to get
very nonsensical results.

818
00:44:55,340 --> 00:44:58,400

819
00:44:58,400 --> 00:45:04,800
OK, so let's move on
to the agentic world.

820
00:45:04,800 --> 00:45:06,480
So you can think
about what we've

821
00:45:06,480 --> 00:45:11,520
been doing so far is evaluate
what language models say,

822
00:45:11,520 --> 00:45:12,380
and that's chat.

823
00:45:12,380 --> 00:45:17,200
And now, we're going to evaluate
what LLMs do, which is agents.

824
00:45:17,200 --> 00:45:24,240
And so for anyone who's
following AI hype,

825
00:45:24,240 --> 00:45:26,340
agents are everywhere.

826
00:45:26,340 --> 00:45:30,440
And these are--
definitely, I think

827
00:45:30,440 --> 00:45:34,960
going to transform the way that
we think about language models.

828
00:45:34,960 --> 00:45:37,290
So Agent is basically
a language model

829
00:45:37,290 --> 00:45:41,050
plus some scaffold, which
is a logic for deciding

830
00:45:41,050 --> 00:45:45,650
how the language model
is going to be called

831
00:45:45,650 --> 00:45:51,970
and what tools it can has access
to and so on and so forth.

832
00:45:51,970 --> 00:45:56,130
So there's a few benchmarks
I want to just point out

833
00:45:56,130 --> 00:45:57,890
that are authentic.

834
00:45:57,890 --> 00:46:01,870
So one is very popular.

835
00:46:01,870 --> 00:46:03,350
It's just called SWE-bench.

836
00:46:03,350 --> 00:46:06,050

837
00:46:06,050 --> 00:46:07,970
Basically, the task
is you're given

838
00:46:07,970 --> 00:46:12,090
a code base and a description
of a GitHub issue, submit a PR.

839
00:46:12,090 --> 00:46:18,530
And the way you evaluate a PR is
whether it passes the unit test.

840
00:46:18,530 --> 00:46:22,770
So these tests are designed so
that before you submit a PR,

841
00:46:22,770 --> 00:46:24,710
certain unit tests don't pass.

842
00:46:24,710 --> 00:46:26,230
And then your goal is to fix it.

843
00:46:26,230 --> 00:46:29,410
So certain unit tests pass and
you don't break anything else.

844
00:46:29,410 --> 00:46:34,000
So here's an example
of some instructions.

845
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:36,260
Here's the issue.

846
00:46:36,260 --> 00:46:39,880
And then some code that is
provided in the context.

847
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:43,220
And then your goal is
to generate a patch.

848
00:46:43,220 --> 00:46:46,820
And given a patch
you are evaluated on

849
00:46:46,820 --> 00:46:48,980
whether you pass or not.

850
00:46:48,980 --> 00:46:51,580
So the good thing about
this is that the evaluation

851
00:46:51,580 --> 00:46:54,100
is very straightforward.

852
00:46:54,100 --> 00:47:00,620
And let's see how we're
doing on SWE-bench.

853
00:47:00,620 --> 00:47:03,420
Actually, this is going to
be SWE-bench Verified which

854
00:47:03,420 --> 00:47:08,500
later I'll show you, tell
you why this happened.

855
00:47:08,500 --> 00:47:12,820
So SWE-bench in--
this was only I

856
00:47:12,820 --> 00:47:22,860
guess 2024 was still around
16% and now it's up to 93.

857
00:47:22,860 --> 00:47:24,040
Good job Mythos.

858
00:47:24,040 --> 00:47:31,280

859
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:36,620
So this is a very canonical
generic benchmark,

860
00:47:36,620 --> 00:47:39,520
as a lot of the
attention on agents

861
00:47:39,520 --> 00:47:42,420
has been invested
in coding abilities.

862
00:47:42,420 --> 00:47:45,920
So SWE-bench really
pioneered this way

863
00:47:45,920 --> 00:47:48,760
of thinking about how to
assess an agent's ability

864
00:47:48,760 --> 00:47:53,800
to write and code in a
realistic environment.

865
00:47:53,800 --> 00:47:58,240
So here's another project
called Terminal-Bench.

866
00:47:58,240 --> 00:48:01,880
And here the idea is
that this is going

867
00:48:01,880 --> 00:48:03,540
to be more general purpose.

868
00:48:03,540 --> 00:48:06,880
And we're going to basically
have the environment be

869
00:48:06,880 --> 00:48:08,600
a computer terminal.

870
00:48:08,600 --> 00:48:11,640
And we're going to
define a bunch of tasks

871
00:48:11,640 --> 00:48:13,640
that can be done in
a computer terminal

872
00:48:13,640 --> 00:48:16,612
by typing in a
bunch of commands.

873
00:48:16,612 --> 00:48:18,320
And the nice thing
about this environment

874
00:48:18,320 --> 00:48:23,000
is a very simple and universal.

875
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:30,970
And these tasks were
crowdsourced from 93 people

876
00:48:30,970 --> 00:48:35,210
from across the world.

877
00:48:35,210 --> 00:48:40,930
89 of these tasks are
constitute Terminal-Bench 2.0.

878
00:48:40,930 --> 00:48:47,170
These tasks generally
take ranging from one hour

879
00:48:47,170 --> 00:48:49,330
to over a week, depending
on whether you're

880
00:48:49,330 --> 00:48:52,250
an expert or a junior.

881
00:48:52,250 --> 00:48:53,930
And then, here's
what the leaderboard

882
00:48:53,930 --> 00:48:57,810
looks like as of today.

883
00:48:57,810 --> 00:49:01,370
So top models are
the frontier models.

884
00:49:01,370 --> 00:49:07,650
One thing that's interesting is
that the agent is also important

885
00:49:07,650 --> 00:49:13,370
here because you can have
two agents but the same model

886
00:49:13,370 --> 00:49:17,690
and they have
different accuracies.

887
00:49:17,690 --> 00:49:23,150
So another benchmark I'll
talk about is Cybersecurity.

888
00:49:23,150 --> 00:49:27,370
So this is 40 Capture
The Flag tasks.

889
00:49:27,370 --> 00:49:30,420
And the setting here
is that you have

890
00:49:30,420 --> 00:49:35,860
an agent that is
given an environment

891
00:49:35,860 --> 00:49:41,220
and it can run various commands
like look at the source code.

892
00:49:41,220 --> 00:49:47,960
It can access the web server.

893
00:49:47,960 --> 00:49:50,380
And the goal is to essentially
hack into this server

894
00:49:50,380 --> 00:49:53,220
to extract some flag, which
is a unique string that

895
00:49:53,220 --> 00:49:56,020
proves that you've
hacked into the server.

896
00:49:56,020 --> 00:49:59,940
So these are competitions,
cybersecurity exercises that

897
00:49:59,940 --> 00:50:02,060
are done by humans normally.

898
00:50:02,060 --> 00:50:11,580
And this is an example
of what the agent

899
00:50:11,580 --> 00:50:13,880
scaffold looks like here.

900
00:50:13,880 --> 00:50:17,060
This is a very simplistic
version where basically you

901
00:50:17,060 --> 00:50:19,580
have one continuous memory.

902
00:50:19,580 --> 00:50:25,180
You produce some action, you
get environment feedback,

903
00:50:25,180 --> 00:50:27,970
and then you put the
results into this buffer

904
00:50:27,970 --> 00:50:29,550
and this is concatenated.

905
00:50:29,550 --> 00:50:33,590
As I'll show you
later, this history

906
00:50:33,590 --> 00:50:35,090
obviously grows quite a bit.

907
00:50:35,090 --> 00:50:41,710
And you'll need to have better
ways of managing context.

908
00:50:41,710 --> 00:50:46,190
So this is what the leaderboard
looks like when it came out.

909
00:50:46,190 --> 00:50:50,390
The best models were
around 10% or so.

910
00:50:50,390 --> 00:50:57,270
And over time now it's
completely solved actually.

911
00:50:57,270 --> 00:50:59,670
So that's CyBench.

912
00:50:59,670 --> 00:51:01,470
There's another
class of problems

913
00:51:01,470 --> 00:51:06,070
which are MLE engineering.

914
00:51:06,070 --> 00:51:11,910
So this benchmark has a
bunch of Kaggle competitions

915
00:51:11,910 --> 00:51:14,550
which involve processing
data, training models,

916
00:51:14,550 --> 00:51:16,110
and so on and so forth.

917
00:51:16,110 --> 00:51:20,770
And the agent can look at the
data set, read the description,

918
00:51:20,770 --> 00:51:26,610
write code, train models, and
you eventually submit something

919
00:51:26,610 --> 00:51:28,690
and you get a grade.

920
00:51:28,690 --> 00:51:32,690
So this is what the leaderboard
looks like right now.

921
00:51:32,690 --> 00:51:37,010
And again, you can see that it's
really the same usual suspects

922
00:51:37,010 --> 00:51:39,210
on the LLM side.

923
00:51:39,210 --> 00:51:41,050
But there's quite
a bit of variation

924
00:51:41,050 --> 00:51:45,530
in terms of the different
agent scaffolds.

925
00:51:45,530 --> 00:51:47,690
So one thing to note
about, I'll point out

926
00:51:47,690 --> 00:51:53,330
about agent scaffolds is
that they matter a lot.

927
00:51:53,330 --> 00:51:58,570
So when I said I talk about
language model evaluation,

928
00:51:58,570 --> 00:52:02,010
this is a broader than
language model evaluation.

929
00:52:02,010 --> 00:52:07,690
And the reason is that unlike
what I mentioned before

930
00:52:07,690 --> 00:52:11,370
with this earliest CyBench
agent scaffold, now

931
00:52:11,370 --> 00:52:14,170
you have to be a lot more
sophisticated to solve

932
00:52:14,170 --> 00:52:16,770
really complicated tasks.

933
00:52:16,770 --> 00:52:20,890
One thing that people realize
is that explicit planning

934
00:52:20,890 --> 00:52:21,950
is helpful.

935
00:52:21,950 --> 00:52:23,940
You can't just stream
of consciousness,

936
00:52:23,940 --> 00:52:24,960
chain of thought this.

937
00:52:24,960 --> 00:52:27,340
And then you build
up this context

938
00:52:27,340 --> 00:52:31,220
and the agent can easily
lose track of where it is.

939
00:52:31,220 --> 00:52:35,580
So instead of keep a to-do
list, get it checked off.

940
00:52:35,580 --> 00:52:40,700
Hierarchical delegation, agents
should call other sub-agents

941
00:52:40,700 --> 00:52:42,160
with some clean context.

942
00:52:42,160 --> 00:52:46,260
And that agent does some stuff
and returns only the result.

943
00:52:46,260 --> 00:52:50,640
The master agent doesn't need
to see all the gory details,

944
00:52:50,640 --> 00:52:55,900
and this can help encapsulate,
provide some encapsulation.

945
00:52:55,900 --> 00:52:59,420
Memory, people have been
experimenting around

946
00:52:59,420 --> 00:53:01,560
with explicitly reading
and writing files,

947
00:53:01,560 --> 00:53:03,400
especially as the context grows.

948
00:53:03,400 --> 00:53:08,580
You can't just store it
in your context window.

949
00:53:08,580 --> 00:53:13,660
And then finally, there is more
context engineering in the style

950
00:53:13,660 --> 00:53:16,260
of managing the process.

951
00:53:16,260 --> 00:53:18,580
When should you
delegate to sub-agents?

952
00:53:18,580 --> 00:53:23,470
When to try this strategy
versus this other strategy, what

953
00:53:23,470 --> 00:53:25,090
to write persistent memory.

954
00:53:25,090 --> 00:53:30,710
And these things are
generally optimized

955
00:53:30,710 --> 00:53:36,470
for the particular
language model.

956
00:53:36,470 --> 00:53:40,670
So agents are exciting because
they enhance the capability

957
00:53:40,670 --> 00:53:42,490
surface of language models.

958
00:53:42,490 --> 00:53:44,130
Scaffolds are very important.

959
00:53:44,130 --> 00:53:47,470
So really, when we talk
about evaluating agents,

960
00:53:47,470 --> 00:53:49,950
it's evaluating both
the language model

961
00:53:49,950 --> 00:53:52,250
and the agent scaffold.

962
00:53:52,250 --> 00:53:56,150

963
00:53:56,150 --> 00:53:58,910
Now, we're going to
switch gears a little bit

964
00:53:58,910 --> 00:54:03,790
and talk about this other very
different style of benchmarks.

965
00:54:03,790 --> 00:54:08,390
I call them pure reasoning
for lack of a better term.

966
00:54:08,390 --> 00:54:11,590
And the motivation is that
all the tasks so far require

967
00:54:11,590 --> 00:54:14,630
some linguistic and
world knowledge.

968
00:54:14,630 --> 00:54:17,070
And so the question
is, can we just

969
00:54:17,070 --> 00:54:21,450
isolate reasoning or
pure fluid intelligence

970
00:54:21,450 --> 00:54:24,330
from a knowledge
and knowing facts?

971
00:54:24,330 --> 00:54:26,730
And you could argue
that this gives you

972
00:54:26,730 --> 00:54:31,410
a form of more
pure intelligence.

973
00:54:31,410 --> 00:54:36,330
So there was this
project, ARC-AGI,

974
00:54:36,330 --> 00:54:43,170
which started back in 2019,
where the goal was exactly this.

975
00:54:43,170 --> 00:54:48,990
So the task was meant to
be 100% solvable by humans,

976
00:54:48,990 --> 00:54:50,490
but challenging for AIs.

977
00:54:50,490 --> 00:54:55,050
And every task is a special
snowflake or that's the intent.

978
00:54:55,050 --> 00:54:57,170
And so memorizing
a bunch of facts

979
00:54:57,170 --> 00:55:00,890
or solving previous
problems shouldn't really

980
00:55:00,890 --> 00:55:02,970
help you that much.

981
00:55:02,970 --> 00:55:06,110
So if you guys are
remembering your LLM history,

982
00:55:06,110 --> 00:55:09,310
2019 was pre-LLM.

983
00:55:09,310 --> 00:55:11,890
I mean this was GPT-2 era.

984
00:55:11,890 --> 00:55:15,140
So this was prescient
in that regard.

985
00:55:15,140 --> 00:55:17,640
So here's an example of the
first iteration of that problem.

986
00:55:17,640 --> 00:55:20,020
You're given these
pictures and you're

987
00:55:20,020 --> 00:55:22,700
trying to guess what comes next.

988
00:55:22,700 --> 00:55:27,080
And if you take 10 seconds, you
can probably solve this problem.

989
00:55:27,080 --> 00:55:30,220

990
00:55:30,220 --> 00:55:33,980
So you probably guess it's
to just fill in the yellow

991
00:55:33,980 --> 00:55:36,540
to form the rectangle.

992
00:55:36,540 --> 00:55:41,980
So then last year, there
was an updated version

993
00:55:41,980 --> 00:55:45,860
of this task, which was
a bit more complicated.

994
00:55:45,860 --> 00:55:47,360
And you can take a look at this.

995
00:55:47,360 --> 00:55:51,220

996
00:55:51,220 --> 00:55:54,640
So here's the trajectory.

997
00:55:54,640 --> 00:55:58,180
So when ARC-AGI-1 came out--

998
00:55:58,180 --> 00:56:01,020

999
00:56:01,020 --> 00:56:03,340
I think this is actually 0.

1000
00:56:03,340 --> 00:56:06,240
So basically models
didn't do anything.

1001
00:56:06,240 --> 00:56:08,100
So there's GPT-3--
pre-trained model didn't

1002
00:56:08,100 --> 00:56:10,580
move the needle at all, right.

1003
00:56:10,580 --> 00:56:14,100
And this was exactly as
the creators had intended.

1004
00:56:14,100 --> 00:56:17,990
Pre-trained models
just learn what

1005
00:56:17,990 --> 00:56:19,910
the facts are on the
internet and maybe learn

1006
00:56:19,910 --> 00:56:21,090
linguistic patterns.

1007
00:56:21,090 --> 00:56:24,570
But none of that is
actually helpful directly,

1008
00:56:24,570 --> 00:56:28,670
at least for these
ARC-AGI tasks.

1009
00:56:28,670 --> 00:56:33,070
And then something
happened in 2024.

1010
00:56:33,070 --> 00:56:36,610
And this is when OpenAI
released their o1 and o3 models.

1011
00:56:36,610 --> 00:56:40,390
Then things started taking
off pretty abruptly.

1012
00:56:40,390 --> 00:56:46,430
And now, ARC-AGI-1
is basically solved.

1013
00:56:46,430 --> 00:56:52,990
And even around 2025
when ARC-AGI-2 came out,

1014
00:56:52,990 --> 00:56:55,970
this has also-- it's
not quite solved,

1015
00:56:55,970 --> 00:57:01,550
but it looks like it's on
its way to being solved.

1016
00:57:01,550 --> 00:57:08,670
So it was really the reasoning
capabilities that unlocked this.

1017
00:57:08,670 --> 00:57:14,120
And now one could wonder
whether all the knowledge

1018
00:57:14,120 --> 00:57:19,780
of actual pre-training was
necessary for this task.

1019
00:57:19,780 --> 00:57:21,800
And certainly,
without pre-training

1020
00:57:21,800 --> 00:57:24,180
we wouldn't have this
explosion of reasoning models.

1021
00:57:24,180 --> 00:57:27,180
So arguably it was
still important,

1022
00:57:27,180 --> 00:57:30,200
but not maybe directly
visible for much

1023
00:57:30,200 --> 00:57:32,800
of the history of ARC-AGI.

1024
00:57:32,800 --> 00:57:36,100
So now, just last month,
ARC-AGI-3 comes out.

1025
00:57:36,100 --> 00:57:37,720
So you see this
familiar pattern.

1026
00:57:37,720 --> 00:57:39,860
Our creators look at
this and it's like, oh,

1027
00:57:39,860 --> 00:57:41,960
time for a new benchmark.

1028
00:57:41,960 --> 00:57:45,880
So this is now an
interactive environment

1029
00:57:45,880 --> 00:57:49,140
where you can go online
and you can play this game.

1030
00:57:49,140 --> 00:57:50,180
So again, no language.

1031
00:57:50,180 --> 00:57:53,440
You're just trying to figure
out, based on patterns.

1032
00:57:53,440 --> 00:57:55,320
It's kind of fun.

1033
00:57:55,320 --> 00:57:57,760
And now the scores
are extremely low.

1034
00:57:57,760 --> 00:58:00,960
So next year when
I teach this class,

1035
00:58:00,960 --> 00:58:03,380
I'm sure I'll have
to update this slide.

1036
00:58:03,380 --> 00:58:05,400
But we'll see.

1037
00:58:05,400 --> 00:58:10,720
So I think this ARC-AGI
line is a really interesting

1038
00:58:10,720 --> 00:58:12,500
to think about.

1039
00:58:12,500 --> 00:58:16,380
The goal is to disentangle
reasoning from knowledge.

1040
00:58:16,380 --> 00:58:18,080
This is really hard to do.

1041
00:58:18,080 --> 00:58:20,197
And it's not--

1042
00:58:20,197 --> 00:58:22,280
I think this is probably
the best attempt at this.

1043
00:58:22,280 --> 00:58:27,820
It's not clear whether you can
really fully decouple things.

1044
00:58:27,820 --> 00:58:32,000
And certainly, if people really
cared about this benchmark,

1045
00:58:32,000 --> 00:58:34,120
I think people probably could--

1046
00:58:34,120 --> 00:58:36,660
people can benchmark I
think probably anything.

1047
00:58:36,660 --> 00:58:38,760
So you could design
questions like,

1048
00:58:38,760 --> 00:58:41,780
are these-- these still
come from some prior

1049
00:58:41,780 --> 00:58:48,300
and not sure there's anything as
quote unquote pure as reasoning.

1050
00:58:48,300 --> 00:58:50,320
This is also constrained
to human reasoning.

1051
00:58:50,320 --> 00:58:53,460
The explicit goal is to
be 100% human solvable

1052
00:58:53,460 --> 00:58:55,780
within some reasonable time.

1053
00:58:55,780 --> 00:58:59,680
So this doesn't really extend
to superhuman reasoning,

1054
00:58:59,680 --> 00:59:02,220
for example [INAUDIBLE]
winning more gold medals

1055
00:59:02,220 --> 00:59:05,820
or solving open math problems,
which arguably are still

1056
00:59:05,820 --> 00:59:07,860
very useful and important.

1057
00:59:07,860 --> 00:59:11,590
But clearly this exposes some
gaps in the current models.

1058
00:59:11,590 --> 00:59:15,310
So there is work to do if
you think about the goal

1059
00:59:15,310 --> 00:59:18,810
as basically being able
to solve all the tasks.

1060
00:59:18,810 --> 00:59:22,990

1061
00:59:22,990 --> 00:59:26,690
So this ARC-AGI-3 especially
looks quite graphic.

1062
00:59:26,690 --> 00:59:32,870
So is there some multimodal
content involved or encoding

1063
00:59:32,870 --> 00:59:36,410
or as a textual
representation as well?

1064
00:59:36,410 --> 00:59:41,350
Yeah so the question is
this looks very graphical.

1065
00:59:41,350 --> 00:59:44,230
And so what is the input
to a language model?

1066
00:59:44,230 --> 00:59:45,710
So this is essentially--

1067
00:59:45,710 --> 00:59:46,950
I don't know what the--

1068
00:59:46,950 --> 00:59:48,930
I think it's 64 by 64.

1069
00:59:48,930 --> 00:59:51,590
So you can provide
this image, or you

1070
00:59:51,590 --> 00:59:58,390
can provide it in Ascii Art or
some of textual representation.

1071
00:59:58,390 --> 01:00:01,750
Either way, there is
a spatial element here

1072
01:00:01,750 --> 01:00:05,830
that the model has to reason
about, which is really not

1073
01:00:05,830 --> 01:00:07,570
English or natural language.

1074
01:00:07,570 --> 01:00:12,920

1075
01:00:12,920 --> 01:00:15,800
So let's talk about
shift gears and talk

1076
01:00:15,800 --> 01:00:18,280
about another
class of benchmarks

1077
01:00:18,280 --> 01:00:21,040
which is related to safety.

1078
01:00:21,040 --> 01:00:23,640
So people have been
talking about safety

1079
01:00:23,640 --> 01:00:25,960
in language models for a while.

1080
01:00:25,960 --> 01:00:29,520
And if you think about
other areas such as cars,

1081
01:00:29,520 --> 01:00:32,280
it's pretty clear what
safety means here.

1082
01:00:32,280 --> 01:00:37,340
These ratings for
safety ratings for cars,

1083
01:00:37,340 --> 01:00:40,440
which involve testing
based on smashing cars

1084
01:00:40,440 --> 01:00:43,040
into a wall with an
airbag and seeing

1085
01:00:43,040 --> 01:00:49,520
how well it does and protects
the dummies in the car.

1086
01:00:49,520 --> 01:00:53,440
And this is with decades of
lobbying and figuring out

1087
01:00:53,440 --> 01:00:55,800
what safety means for vehicles.

1088
01:00:55,800 --> 01:00:57,760
So what does it mean for AI?

1089
01:00:57,760 --> 01:01:01,523
So I would say that there
is not a great answer here,

1090
01:01:01,523 --> 01:01:03,440
but I'll give you some
examples of what people

1091
01:01:03,440 --> 01:01:05,080
have been thinking about.

1092
01:01:05,080 --> 01:01:08,760
So there's this benchmark
called Harm-Bench.

1093
01:01:08,760 --> 01:01:14,140
And this is really about
prompting a model with things

1094
01:01:14,140 --> 01:01:20,060
that are meant to be harmful
and expecting the model

1095
01:01:20,060 --> 01:01:22,940
to essentially refuse.

1096
01:01:22,940 --> 01:01:25,400
So this is one type of safety.

1097
01:01:25,400 --> 01:01:28,780
And I think probably
the dominant thing

1098
01:01:28,780 --> 01:01:30,740
when people think
about safety is

1099
01:01:30,740 --> 01:01:34,020
that I want to prevent bad
actors from doing things

1100
01:01:34,020 --> 01:01:35,860
with language models,
but it turns out

1101
01:01:35,860 --> 01:01:38,080
that's not the only
thing that matters.

1102
01:01:38,080 --> 01:01:39,860
So there's this other
work, AIR-Bench,

1103
01:01:39,860 --> 01:01:44,460
which tries to think
about safety holistically

1104
01:01:44,460 --> 01:01:49,940
by looking at all the regulatory
frameworks in the EU, China,

1105
01:01:49,940 --> 01:01:55,140
and the US company
policies and builds

1106
01:01:55,140 --> 01:02:00,980
a taxonomy of all the different
things that could go wrong.

1107
01:02:00,980 --> 01:02:03,220
And then using
this, constructing

1108
01:02:03,220 --> 01:02:08,470
a particular set of
prompts and evaluating

1109
01:02:08,470 --> 01:02:12,030
and so on and so forth.

1110
01:02:12,030 --> 01:02:15,730
But one problem with
safety is, jailbreaking,

1111
01:02:15,730 --> 01:02:18,470
which is its own
sub problem, which

1112
01:02:18,470 --> 01:02:20,990
is that language models
are generally trained

1113
01:02:20,990 --> 01:02:23,790
to refuse harmful instructions.

1114
01:02:23,790 --> 01:02:28,590
But as we know you can get
around this if you're clever.

1115
01:02:28,590 --> 01:02:30,910
And so there's some
early work that

1116
01:02:30,910 --> 01:02:37,630
uses automatic ways to optimize
for prompts to bypass safety

1117
01:02:37,630 --> 01:02:43,230
using GCG, which is
basically this coordinate

1118
01:02:43,230 --> 01:02:45,350
wise optimization algorithm.

1119
01:02:45,350 --> 01:02:49,990
And remarkably,
optimizing on open models

1120
01:02:49,990 --> 01:02:51,690
allows us to transfer to models.

1121
01:02:51,690 --> 01:02:55,490
So I hope this doesn't these
attacks don't work anymore.

1122
01:02:55,490 --> 01:02:59,510
But at the time, you can
generate some basically

1123
01:02:59,510 --> 01:03:05,180
gibberish and step by step
plan to destroy humanity

1124
01:03:05,180 --> 01:03:10,240
and OpenAI's model will comply.

1125
01:03:10,240 --> 01:03:11,860
Obviously, you
could argue, well,

1126
01:03:11,860 --> 01:03:16,960
is this actually harmful
plan to destroy humanity?

1127
01:03:16,960 --> 01:03:19,840
At least it did not-- the
expected behavior is that it

1128
01:03:19,840 --> 01:03:22,240
should probably refuse that.

1129
01:03:22,240 --> 01:03:28,680
So this raises a bigger problem
which is, what is safety?

1130
01:03:28,680 --> 01:03:30,720
And one thing that
makes this tricky

1131
01:03:30,720 --> 01:03:35,240
is that many aspects of
safety are very contextual.

1132
01:03:35,240 --> 01:03:39,940
So it involves politics,
law, social norms.

1133
01:03:39,940 --> 01:03:43,120
This varies across
different countries.

1134
01:03:43,120 --> 01:03:45,580
And then also, if you
think about safety,

1135
01:03:45,580 --> 01:03:48,680
you have to think about
what are the risks.

1136
01:03:48,680 --> 01:03:53,480
And the risks are actually
quite varied as well

1137
01:03:53,480 --> 01:03:55,820
and demand probably
different attention.

1138
01:03:55,820 --> 01:03:58,520
So hallucinations is
a risk, especially

1139
01:03:58,520 --> 01:04:03,180
let's say a medical or
legal or financial settings.

1140
01:04:03,180 --> 01:04:06,720
But this is also correlated
with just capabilities.

1141
01:04:06,720 --> 01:04:09,340
If you make a model
more accurate,

1142
01:04:09,340 --> 01:04:12,060
it's correlated with not
who is seeing as much.

1143
01:04:12,060 --> 01:04:15,220
Sycophancy is a problem.

1144
01:04:15,220 --> 01:04:16,920
Abetting crimes is a problem.

1145
01:04:16,920 --> 01:04:23,500
But this might be counter
to capabilities, inequality.

1146
01:04:23,500 --> 01:04:29,140
Losing critical thinking might
be a risk as well that correlate

1147
01:04:29,140 --> 01:04:30,040
with capability.

1148
01:04:30,040 --> 01:04:35,060
So if you think about the
societal impact of language

1149
01:04:35,060 --> 01:04:39,180
models on the world
and think about safety

1150
01:04:39,180 --> 01:04:42,680
as ensuring that AI
goes well for people,

1151
01:04:42,680 --> 01:04:46,580
this is actually a
much more complex topic

1152
01:04:46,580 --> 01:04:48,800
than I'll have
time to talk about.

1153
01:04:48,800 --> 01:04:53,240
And finally, there is a dual
use aspect of language models.

1154
01:04:53,240 --> 01:04:56,860
So the cybersecurity agents can
be used to hack into a system

1155
01:04:56,860 --> 01:05:00,630
or to do penetration testing and
make systems more secure, which

1156
01:05:00,630 --> 01:05:04,630
makes talking about whether
these are safety risks

1157
01:05:04,630 --> 01:05:09,750
or actually beneficial
a double-edged sword.

1158
01:05:09,750 --> 01:05:14,990
OK, so let's move on now
to talk about some broader

1159
01:05:14,990 --> 01:05:17,610
considerations of evaluation.

1160
01:05:17,610 --> 01:05:23,790
So one thing that I think is an
important aspect of evaluation

1161
01:05:23,790 --> 01:05:27,390
is realism or
ecological validity.

1162
01:05:27,390 --> 01:05:30,390
How well does evaluation
capture real-world use?

1163
01:05:30,390 --> 01:05:37,230
So in particular exams are very
far away from real-world use.

1164
01:05:37,230 --> 01:05:40,430
Chatbot Arena are
from real people

1165
01:05:40,430 --> 01:05:44,390
but one could wonder
whether this distribution is

1166
01:05:44,390 --> 01:05:48,510
the right distribution of
people or use cases or not.

1167
01:05:48,510 --> 01:05:51,030
There's a few
benchmarks I'll mention

1168
01:05:51,030 --> 01:05:55,170
that try to get at
ecological validity,

1169
01:05:55,170 --> 01:05:58,140
and in particular at
the use case level,

1170
01:05:58,140 --> 01:06:00,400
at least not the
individual query level.

1171
01:06:00,400 --> 01:06:05,640
So OpenAI created
benchmark called GDPVal.

1172
01:06:05,640 --> 01:06:08,640
They looked at all
the top nine sectors

1173
01:06:08,640 --> 01:06:13,080
according to US GDP, 44
different occupations,

1174
01:06:13,080 --> 01:06:17,080
got a bunch of professionals
to create tasks.

1175
01:06:17,080 --> 01:06:20,920
These are professionals with
about 14 years of experience.

1176
01:06:20,920 --> 01:06:25,840
And you can see people who
are nurses who work concierge,

1177
01:06:25,840 --> 01:06:28,960
real estate agents,
film and video editors

1178
01:06:28,960 --> 01:06:30,600
and so on and so forth.

1179
01:06:30,600 --> 01:06:35,360
And they created
a bunch of tasks.

1180
01:06:35,360 --> 01:06:37,520
Here's another example.

1181
01:06:37,520 --> 01:06:42,100
In the medical domain
where up until this point,

1182
01:06:42,100 --> 01:06:45,640
a lot of medical benchmarks were
based on standardized exams.

1183
01:06:45,640 --> 01:06:47,620
And in fact,
actually for humans,

1184
01:06:47,620 --> 01:06:50,180
this is also what happens.

1185
01:06:50,180 --> 01:06:51,800
You take these
standardized exams

1186
01:06:51,800 --> 01:06:56,242
and you graduate from med school
and then you go operate on.

1187
01:06:56,242 --> 01:06:57,950
Well, actually, there's
a longer process.

1188
01:06:57,950 --> 01:06:59,790
You don't operate on
patients directly.

1189
01:06:59,790 --> 01:07:03,810

1190
01:07:03,810 --> 01:07:06,470
But the point is that
for a language model,

1191
01:07:06,470 --> 01:07:09,290
you should also not
just pass medical exams

1192
01:07:09,290 --> 01:07:11,830
and deploy them directly.

1193
01:07:11,830 --> 01:07:18,490
So this was a project
where 121 tasks were

1194
01:07:18,490 --> 01:07:21,610
sourced from 29 clinicians.

1195
01:07:21,610 --> 01:07:25,410
And this was meant to
represent the type of things

1196
01:07:25,410 --> 01:07:28,450
that clinicians would actually
ask to a language model, which

1197
01:07:28,450 --> 01:07:31,490
are quite different
than the multiple choice

1198
01:07:31,490 --> 01:07:34,410
type of exam questions.

1199
01:07:34,410 --> 01:07:39,870
Here's another project
which gets at real data.

1200
01:07:39,870 --> 01:07:43,010
So if you ask, who
actually has the data

1201
01:07:43,010 --> 01:07:44,230
on what people are doing?

1202
01:07:44,230 --> 01:07:46,810
Well, it's the model developers.

1203
01:07:46,810 --> 01:07:48,970
And so this project
is interesting

1204
01:07:48,970 --> 01:07:55,750
because it uses language models
themselves to analyze real data.

1205
01:07:55,750 --> 01:07:58,490
So in particular,
you do to privacy.

1206
01:07:58,490 --> 01:08:02,370
You can't literally go
and look at people's data,

1207
01:08:02,370 --> 01:08:04,990
but you can have language
models look at the data

1208
01:08:04,990 --> 01:08:08,030
and analyze and summarize
general patterns.

1209
01:08:08,030 --> 01:08:10,750
So then the language
models could tell you

1210
01:08:10,750 --> 01:08:13,990
about what are the
types of people--

1211
01:08:13,990 --> 01:08:17,450
what are the types of things
that people use Claude for?

1212
01:08:17,450 --> 01:08:20,470

1213
01:08:20,470 --> 01:08:24,350
And sometimes
realism and privacy

1214
01:08:24,350 --> 01:08:27,109
are at odds with each
other because ideally you

1215
01:08:27,109 --> 01:08:31,609
would want actually a sample
from the actual query stream.

1216
01:08:31,609 --> 01:08:33,990
And that would be very
valuable to assess

1217
01:08:33,990 --> 01:08:36,350
how well a model is working
and understand deeply

1218
01:08:36,350 --> 01:08:37,510
what the errors are.

1219
01:08:37,510 --> 01:08:43,830
But then that runs into
privacy considerations.

1220
01:08:43,830 --> 01:08:46,670
OK, so another
consideration, which

1221
01:08:46,670 --> 01:08:51,899
is another type of validity
is just in general,

1222
01:08:51,899 --> 01:08:55,880
how do that the evaluations
are scientifically valid?

1223
01:08:55,880 --> 01:08:59,920
And this was brought up fairly
early, train-test overlap

1224
01:08:59,920 --> 01:09:01,680
or contamination.

1225
01:09:01,680 --> 01:09:04,319
So in a machine
learning 101, you

1226
01:09:04,319 --> 01:09:08,960
learn don't train on
your test set hopefully.

1227
01:09:08,960 --> 01:09:11,279
And before foundation
models this

1228
01:09:11,279 --> 01:09:15,060
was actually pretty clear right.

1229
01:09:15,060 --> 01:09:19,080
So with ImageNet and
SQuAD and many benchmarks

1230
01:09:19,080 --> 01:09:21,560
sorry, data sets, there
was always a train split

1231
01:09:21,560 --> 01:09:24,380
and a test split and everyone
played the same game.

1232
01:09:24,380 --> 01:09:28,680
You train on your training data,
you test on your test data.

1233
01:09:28,680 --> 01:09:30,160
Life was simple.

1234
01:09:30,160 --> 01:09:32,240
And now, you have
these models that

1235
01:09:32,240 --> 01:09:34,300
are trained on the internet.

1236
01:09:34,300 --> 01:09:36,880
And much more than that.

1237
01:09:36,880 --> 01:09:39,700
And furthermore, you
don't what's in the data.

1238
01:09:39,700 --> 01:09:44,520
So this makes evaluation
quite different I think.

1239
01:09:44,520 --> 01:09:46,960
So what can you do in this?

1240
01:09:46,960 --> 01:09:50,810
So first thing you
can do is you can

1241
01:09:50,810 --> 01:09:56,970
try to infer whether a model
has actually seen the test data.

1242
01:09:56,970 --> 01:10:03,410
So this is a neat idea
from Tatsu's group

1243
01:10:03,410 --> 01:10:07,890
where you leverage the
fact that the order

1244
01:10:07,890 --> 01:10:11,650
in which the questions
in the benchmark occurs

1245
01:10:11,650 --> 01:10:14,130
should be random.

1246
01:10:14,130 --> 01:10:19,210
But if the model prefers
some particular order that's

1247
01:10:19,210 --> 01:10:22,010
consistent with the
benchmark, that is probably--

1248
01:10:22,010 --> 01:10:26,970
it's just trained
on that benchmark.

1249
01:10:26,970 --> 01:10:35,770
Another route is that you can
encourage people to report

1250
01:10:35,770 --> 01:10:38,370
actual train-test overlap.

1251
01:10:38,370 --> 01:10:41,090
So this has to do with norms.

1252
01:10:41,090 --> 01:10:44,790
I think in general, especially
maybe less in machine learning,

1253
01:10:44,790 --> 01:10:47,550
but more in
statistics you always

1254
01:10:47,550 --> 01:10:50,070
report confidence intervals.

1255
01:10:50,070 --> 01:10:51,010
You have an estimate.

1256
01:10:51,010 --> 01:10:53,890
And I always want to know,
how reliable is that estimate?

1257
01:10:53,890 --> 01:10:55,630
This is just standard practice.

1258
01:10:55,630 --> 01:10:59,190
And so the idea of
this position paper

1259
01:10:59,190 --> 01:11:02,010
is that you should
model providers.

1260
01:11:02,010 --> 01:11:06,030
If you're going to
claim some GPQA score,

1261
01:11:06,030 --> 01:11:09,870
you should always provide
some justification

1262
01:11:09,870 --> 01:11:13,490
that you didn't train
on the test set.

1263
01:11:13,490 --> 01:11:16,750

1264
01:11:16,750 --> 01:11:19,970
So another route is that
where you can just give up.

1265
01:11:19,970 --> 01:11:23,863
It's like OK, all these
evaluations there--

1266
01:11:23,863 --> 01:11:26,030
let's just assume that
people trained on them, let's

1267
01:11:26,030 --> 01:11:28,990
assume the worst case.

1268
01:11:28,990 --> 01:11:31,270
So the thing that
we have on our side

1269
01:11:31,270 --> 01:11:33,570
is that you can always
define fresh evals.

1270
01:11:33,570 --> 01:11:38,070
So there's a bunch of ideas,
LiveCodeBench, UncheeatableEval,

1271
01:11:38,070 --> 01:11:40,910
which basically scrape
new web pages or archive

1272
01:11:40,910 --> 01:11:46,520
papers or GitHub and defines
evaluations populations that

1273
01:11:46,520 --> 01:11:50,760
are past the cutoff date
of the language models.

1274
01:11:50,760 --> 01:11:54,840
So this seems pretty
robust and good.

1275
01:11:54,840 --> 01:11:57,360
But one thing to keep in
mind is that time stamps

1276
01:11:57,360 --> 01:12:00,880
aren't always safe
either, because I might

1277
01:12:00,880 --> 01:12:05,220
post with code for example.

1278
01:12:05,220 --> 01:12:08,280
I might put something on
GitHub which is actually

1279
01:12:08,280 --> 01:12:11,320
derived from some other repo.

1280
01:12:11,320 --> 01:12:14,180
And so how do that's
actually generally fresh?

1281
01:12:14,180 --> 01:12:17,360

1282
01:12:17,360 --> 01:12:21,140
Route 4 is to use
private evaluations.

1283
01:12:21,140 --> 01:12:23,860
So companies' model
developers do this right.

1284
01:12:23,860 --> 01:12:26,520
So if you're Google or OpenAI
you have your internal code

1285
01:12:26,520 --> 01:12:29,340
base, which you assume
is not on the internet,

1286
01:12:29,340 --> 01:12:34,840
and you obviously wouldn't
train on this data,

1287
01:12:34,840 --> 01:12:37,000
so you can use it
for evaluation.

1288
01:12:37,000 --> 01:12:40,400
So this generally
is pretty reliable.

1289
01:12:40,400 --> 01:12:44,610
And if you're not a company, you
can use your personal writings.

1290
01:12:44,610 --> 01:12:47,410
I have a bunch of
rejected papers from when

1291
01:12:47,410 --> 01:12:49,950
I was in grad school that
I never put on online,

1292
01:12:49,950 --> 01:12:52,490
so I can use those.

1293
01:12:52,490 --> 01:12:56,590
And this type of
evaluation, I think,

1294
01:12:56,590 --> 01:13:00,130
is very favorable
for perplexity evals,

1295
01:13:00,130 --> 01:13:01,770
because all you
need in perplexity

1296
01:13:01,770 --> 01:13:04,970
is just you need a
good data set and you

1297
01:13:04,970 --> 01:13:09,130
can evaluate log probabilities.

1298
01:13:09,130 --> 01:13:16,970
So another concern that I'll
mention is data set quality.

1299
01:13:16,970 --> 01:13:19,650
So remember I showed
the leaderboard

1300
01:13:19,650 --> 01:13:22,610
for SWE-Bench Verified.

1301
01:13:22,610 --> 01:13:26,010
This is because,
well, SWE-Bench had

1302
01:13:26,010 --> 01:13:30,970
a bunch of problems of
tasks, unit tests that

1303
01:13:30,970 --> 01:13:36,770
weren't quite rigorous enough
and other things that got fixed.

1304
01:13:36,770 --> 01:13:44,150
And there are many other
benchmarks that have gone

1305
01:13:44,150 --> 01:13:49,070
through this process
like GSM8K or MMLU,

1306
01:13:49,070 --> 01:13:54,670
where people have gone and done
a more careful audit and realize

1307
01:13:54,670 --> 01:13:59,230
that, wait a minute, this
question actually doesn't--

1308
01:13:59,230 --> 01:14:02,510
something's broken
around this question.

1309
01:14:02,510 --> 01:14:04,090
So like this one.

1310
01:14:04,090 --> 01:14:07,410
There's no curve
given in this text

1311
01:14:07,410 --> 01:14:09,210
so you can't answer
this question.

1312
01:14:09,210 --> 01:14:12,190

1313
01:14:12,190 --> 01:14:14,310
Does the baby have socks on?

1314
01:14:14,310 --> 01:14:16,230
No, there's no way to tell.

1315
01:14:16,230 --> 01:14:21,623
And there's also this paper
that showed that there's

1316
01:14:21,623 --> 01:14:23,790
a lot of problems with the
agentic benchmarks, which

1317
01:14:23,790 --> 01:14:26,790
arguably are even harder to
assess because it's not just

1318
01:14:26,790 --> 01:14:30,190
you can look at the question
and the possible answers

1319
01:14:30,190 --> 01:14:34,110
and you can look at it but
there's a whole environment.

1320
01:14:34,110 --> 01:14:39,190
And there are cases
where with coding you can

1321
01:14:39,190 --> 01:14:43,340
it's great to have test cases
that either pass or not passed.

1322
01:14:43,340 --> 01:14:46,560
But as we all test
cases can be incomplete.

1323
01:14:46,560 --> 01:14:48,120
So you might pass
all the test cases

1324
01:14:48,120 --> 01:14:50,960
but still not have
a working solution.

1325
01:14:50,960 --> 01:14:53,520
There's another case of
this TorchBench benchmark

1326
01:14:53,520 --> 01:14:57,780
where they show that if an agent
just outputs the empty response,

1327
01:14:57,780 --> 01:15:00,280
they can get a 38%.

1328
01:15:00,280 --> 01:15:03,600
So there are some trivialities
with these benchmarks

1329
01:15:03,600 --> 01:15:07,095
that you want have to be
very careful to avoid.

1330
01:15:07,095 --> 01:15:08,720
One thing I'll call
out is that there's

1331
01:15:08,720 --> 01:15:12,880
this tool called
Docent that uses

1332
01:15:12,880 --> 01:15:17,480
language models to inspect
agent traces to detect problems.

1333
01:15:17,480 --> 01:15:19,600
So this is a
qualitative response

1334
01:15:19,600 --> 01:15:23,800
to our very quantitative
heavy way of benchmarking.

1335
01:15:23,800 --> 01:15:25,960
And I would always
recommend that whenever

1336
01:15:25,960 --> 01:15:29,200
you do a benchmark,
either develop a benchmark

1337
01:15:29,200 --> 01:15:33,680
or run a model on the
benchmark that you always

1338
01:15:33,680 --> 01:15:36,855
look at the output and try
to audit it to make sure

1339
01:15:36,855 --> 01:15:38,730
that you actually think
you're measuring what

1340
01:15:38,730 --> 01:15:41,650
you think you're measuring.

1341
01:15:41,650 --> 01:15:45,670
OK, so maybe getting a
bit more philosophical.

1342
01:15:45,670 --> 01:15:47,610
So what's the point
of evaluation?

1343
01:15:47,610 --> 01:15:48,110
OK.

1344
01:15:48,110 --> 01:15:51,650
So I've talked a lot about
different types of evaluation.

1345
01:15:51,650 --> 01:15:55,670
And really, there's no one
evaluation to rule them all.

1346
01:15:55,670 --> 01:15:58,290
And I think it's-- when you're
thinking about evaluation,

1347
01:15:58,290 --> 01:16:01,830
you have to be very clear
about what the purpose is.

1348
01:16:01,830 --> 01:16:05,410
And I think often this is
not really stated clearly.

1349
01:16:05,410 --> 01:16:08,590
So for example, you
could be a user,

1350
01:16:08,590 --> 01:16:11,110
or a company that's trying
to make a purchase decision.

1351
01:16:11,110 --> 01:16:14,850
Are you going to go with A or
B for a particular use case?

1352
01:16:14,850 --> 01:16:17,010
Or you're just a
researcher and you

1353
01:16:17,010 --> 01:16:19,730
have this intuitive
notion of intelligence

1354
01:16:19,730 --> 01:16:22,330
and you want to
measure it somehow.

1355
01:16:22,330 --> 01:16:25,210
Or you want to understand the
benefits and harms for business

1356
01:16:25,210 --> 01:16:26,650
or policy reasons.

1357
01:16:26,650 --> 01:16:28,890
Or you're a developer and
you want to get feedback

1358
01:16:28,890 --> 01:16:30,930
to improve the model.

1359
01:16:30,930 --> 01:16:33,050
So each of these
goals will lead you

1360
01:16:33,050 --> 01:16:38,300
to potentially different
benchmarks or some combination

1361
01:16:38,300 --> 01:16:41,060
of evaluation strategies.

1362
01:16:41,060 --> 01:16:43,560
And finally, one
thing to address,

1363
01:16:43,560 --> 01:16:45,380
which I hinted at
briefly, is that

1364
01:16:45,380 --> 01:16:47,080
what are we even evaluating?

1365
01:16:47,080 --> 01:16:51,820
So before foundation models,
researchers evaluated methods

1366
01:16:51,820 --> 01:16:53,960
because we have these
trained test splits.

1367
01:16:53,960 --> 01:16:57,780
And the only thing that was
varying is the actual algorithm.

1368
01:16:57,780 --> 01:17:01,020
And today, we're mostly
evaluating models and systems

1369
01:17:01,020 --> 01:17:03,300
where anything goes.

1370
01:17:03,300 --> 01:17:04,800
There are some exceptions.

1371
01:17:04,800 --> 01:17:08,180
So the NanoGPT speedrun
is deliberately

1372
01:17:08,180 --> 01:17:12,300
a way to evaluate an algorithm,
in particular, how well fast you

1373
01:17:12,300 --> 01:17:14,000
can train a model.

1374
01:17:14,000 --> 01:17:17,820
But most of language
model evaluation

1375
01:17:17,820 --> 01:17:22,180
is about evaluating the actual
end model, which is useful

1376
01:17:22,180 --> 01:17:25,100
because that is the thing that
is actually going to get shipped

1377
01:17:25,100 --> 01:17:26,680
and people are going to use.

1378
01:17:26,680 --> 01:17:29,740
But I think the punchline
is that you should always

1379
01:17:29,740 --> 01:17:37,480
be very deliberate about
declaring what your goals are.

1380
01:17:37,480 --> 01:17:40,700
OK, so there's no
one true evaluation.

1381
01:17:40,700 --> 01:17:44,480
Choose what you're
trying to measure.

1382
01:17:44,480 --> 01:17:48,680
And we talked about a bunch
of different benchmarks

1383
01:17:48,680 --> 01:17:53,560
starting with perplexity, exam
based, chat, agentic, reasoning,

1384
01:17:53,560 --> 01:17:55,440
and safety.

1385
01:17:55,440 --> 01:18:00,080
They vary in terms of
difficulty, realism,

1386
01:18:00,080 --> 01:18:01,280
how valid they are.

1387
01:18:01,280 --> 01:18:02,580
And there's often trade offs.

1388
01:18:02,580 --> 01:18:07,600
It's hard to have really real
and difficult and ecologically

1389
01:18:07,600 --> 01:18:08,140
valid.

1390
01:18:08,140 --> 01:18:10,060
And no trade tests
contamination.

1391
01:18:10,060 --> 01:18:13,300
So often you have to compromise
on one of these factors,

1392
01:18:13,300 --> 01:18:15,080
and depending on
your goals, you will

1393
01:18:15,080 --> 01:18:19,200
have to choose which one you're
willing to compromise on.

1394
01:18:19,200 --> 01:18:22,300
So that is the end
of this lecture.

1395
01:18:22,300 --> 01:18:23,760
So next week, we're
going to start

1396
01:18:23,760 --> 01:18:26,520
talking about training data.

1397
01:18:26,520 --> 01:18:27,660
OK, see you next time.

1398
01:18:27,660 --> 01:18:30,710
[AUDIO LOGO]

1399
01:18:30,710 --> 01:18:34,000

